{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785179079462,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"task","callID":"call_vufJ0yO2hGMXPBMnllFppThu","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"description":"Research git operation inventory","subagent_type":"deep-research","command":"/deep:research:auto","prompt":"Execute exactly ONE deep-research LEAF iteration for the detached lineage below. Do not dispatch subagents.\n\nResearch topic: Determine which git operations warrant a preflight advisory in a PreToolUse Bash hook, what pre-execution state each candidate rule can evaluate, and where the noise threshold sits.\nIteration: 1 of 5\nFocus: Inventory command-classifiable git operation families and map each to pre-execution state that can actually be read before execution. Go beyond the briefing's five incidents. Distinguish command-only predicates from stateful checks and identify impossible post-execution-only checks.\n\nRead state FIRST:\n- .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-config.json\n- .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-state.jsonl\n- .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n- .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md\n\nPrimary evidence:\n- .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md\n- .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs\n- .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/SKILL.md\n- bounded local git history/command evidence where useful\n\nRequired finding contract from BRIEFING.md: every finding MUST state exact operation shape, specific pre-execution state, whether that state is genuinely available before execution, noise estimate measured from real repository history or explicit 'not measured', source classification (named sk-git rule, observed incident, or new with justification), and confidence as confirmed or inferred. Findings that merely restate the five incidents are low value.\n\nAllowed write paths, and no others:\n- .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-001.md\n- append exactly one canonical iteration record to .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-state.jsonl\n- .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-001.jsonl\n\nDo not edit config, strategy, registry, dashboard, research.md, source files, parent packet files, or any path outside the lineage. Do not stage or commit.\n\nThe iteration state and delta record must include: type=iteration, iteration=1, run=1, mode=research, target_agent=deep-research, agent_definition_loaded=true, resolved_route='Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research', status, focus, findingsCount, newInfoRatio, noveltyJustification, keyQuestions, answeredQuestions, ruledOut, toolsUsed, sourcesQueried, timestamp, durationMs, graphEvents. Return a concise verification summary only after all three artifacts exist."},"output":"<task id=\"ses_05b0fd535ffelo4TjJd4mFhpfG\" state=\"completed\">\n<task_result>\n## Iteration 1 Complete\n\n**Focus**: Git operation families and genuinely available pre-execution state  \n**Findings**: 12 findings (10 new, 2 partially new)  \n**newInfoRatio**: 0.92  \n**Questions answered**: Direct Git operation families classifiable before execution  \n**Questions remaining**: 4  \n**Edge cases**: Missing exact PreToolUse noise telemetry  \n**Recommended next focus**: Validate bounded state probes and measure match rates\n\n**Files written**:\n- `iterations/iteration-001.md`\n- `deep-research-state.jsonl` — exactly one iteration appended\n- `deltas/iter-001.jsonl`\n\n**Verification**:\n- All three artifacts exist: yes\n- JSONL iteration append count: 1\n- Delta first line byte-equivalent: yes\n- All 12 findings cited: yes\n- Packet boundary respected: yes\n- Reducer-owned files untouched: yes\n\n**Status**: complete\n</task_result>\n</task>","metadata":{"parentSessionId":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","sessionId":"ses_05b0fd535ffelo4TjJd4mFhpfG","model":{"modelID":"gpt-5.6-sol","providerID":"openai"},"truncated":false},"title":"Research git operation inventory","time":{"start":1785178631091,"end":1785179079456}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0fc80bec17ca7f6c016a67a9f8e45c81919a47ac4f0f65fbba"}},"id":"prt_fa4eff443001E9O7iamqpcRPXZ","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa4efdfc6001M7HacPlz18TMAA"}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1785179080681,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"id":"prt_fa4f707d3001RXOgK8Vs1qZ3c6","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"fe03b20dde9c4dbba7dc0e6f79ecce2c3c838fcc","messageID":"msg_fa4efdfc6001M7HacPlz18TMAA","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","type":"step-finish","tokens":{"total":217901,"input":194100,"output":761,"reasoning":0,"cache":{"write":0,"read":23040}},"cost":0}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1785179086580,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"id":"prt_fa4f71ef1001okhL5srUvjk3y2","messageID":"msg_fa4f70c3f001os0vM15SqKAiqm","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","snapshot":"fe03b20dde9c4dbba7dc0e6f79ecce2c3c838fcc","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785179091087,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_XseSKAhLA7KZZxwwIf8Vm6Jt","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-001.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-001.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Iteration 1: Git Operation Families and Pre-Execution State\n2: \n3: ## Focus\n4: Inventory direct `git` invocation families that a Bash `PreToolUse` hook can classify before execution, then separate command-only predicates from checks that need repository, worktree, index, ref, remote, or config reads. The selected interpretation is limited to direct shell-visible Git shapes; aliases, shell functions, commands assembled by `eval`, and arbitrary scripts are deferred because classifying their eventual Git subprocess is not reliable from the original Bash text.\n5: \n6: ## Findings\n7: \n8: The current evaluator is a command-string engine: `evaluate(command, rules)` passes only the composed command to pure check functions. It can host command-only predicates today, but every stateful candidate below would require the PreToolUse path to add bounded read-only probes and a trustworthy command working directory. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:68-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:101-125]\n9: \n10: 1. **Direct branch creation — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch <new> [<start>]`, `git checkout -b|-B <new> [<start>]`, or `git switch -c|-C <new> [<start>]` (including direct `git -C <repo> ...` forms). **Pre-execution state:** the command shape is sufficient for the named prohibition; branch existence and registered worktrees are optional refinements obtainable with `git show-ref --verify` and `git worktree list --porcelain`. **Genuinely available:** yes for a direct invocation; no for a Git command hidden in an opaque script or alias expansion. **Noise:** not measured—Git reflogs do not preserve these argv forms, and only 7/1,482 HEAD-reflog entries were checkouts, which is not a branch-creation count. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #2; it requires branch creation through the allocator-backed `git worktree add -b` flow. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule and command classifier; inferred for operational fire rate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-221] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-486] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n11: \n12: 2. **Blanket hook bypass — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git commit ... --no-verify` or `git push ... --no-verify`, accepting option placement before or after the subcommand's ordinary arguments. **Pre-execution state:** only the parsed presence of `--no-verify`; repository state is not needed. **Genuinely available:** yes for direct commands. **Noise:** not measured because reflogs and commit history omit invocation options. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #9; this is especially advisory-worthy because the operation disables the enforcement layer that would otherwise own the check. **Confidence:** confirmed. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-493] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:46-55]\n13: \n14: 3. **Amending published or integrated HEAD — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit --amend ...` (including `--amend --no-edit`). **Pre-execution state:** resolve `HEAD`; test whether a local remote-tracking ref or protected/integration branch contains that commit (`git for-each-ref --contains HEAD refs/remotes refs/heads`), and identify the current branch/worktree. **Genuinely available:** locally yes, but only relative to possibly stale remote-tracking refs; whether another clone has consumed the commit is not knowable pre-execution. **Noise:** 7 amend events among 1,482 current-worktree HEAD-reflog entries (0.47%); the hazardous published subset was not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #10. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule, available local state, and repository count; inferred for publication completeness. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:492-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n15: \n16: 4. **Rebasing published/shared history — stateful advisory, not a generic rebase warning.** **Operation:** `git rebase ...`, `git pull --rebase ...`, or `git pull ...` when `pull.rebase=true`; include an autostash dimension when `--autostash`, `rebase.autoStash=true`, or `merge.autoStash=true` applies. **Pre-execution state:** current branch/detachment, remote-tracking refs containing the commits to rewrite, ahead/behind counts, dirty-path count, unmerged entries, and relevant Git config. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; remote freshness and concurrent consumers remain unknown. **Noise:** 44 rebase events among 1,482 HEAD-reflog entries (2.97%); an unconditional rebase warning would therefore be materially noisier than a published-history-only predicate. The exact autostash subset was not measured because reflogs omit flags/config. **Source classification:** named sk-git rules NEVER #8 and ALWAYS #14; it extends, rather than restates, the briefing incident by gating on publication state as well as dirty-tree/autostash risk. **Confidence:** confirmed for local state and measured broad rate; inferred for the final combined predicate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:491-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n17: \n18: 5. **Discarding worktree/index content — stateful advisory across four exact shapes.** **Operation:** `git reset --hard [<tree-ish>]`, `git clean -f|-fd|-fdx [-- <pathspec>...]`, `git checkout -- <pathspec>...`, or `git restore --worktree [--source=<tree-ish>] [-- <pathspec>...]`. **Pre-execution state:** pathspec-resolved tracked modifications, staged changes that `reset --hard` would replace, matching untracked/ignored paths for `clean`, and unmerged entries. **Genuinely available:** yes via read-only diffs/status plus Git's own pathspec semantics before execution; exact shell-expanded paths must be parsed first. **Noise:** the reflog contains 201 reset events (13.56%), but does not record reset mode, so 13.56% is only a broad upper bound; `clean`, path checkout, and restore frequency were not measured. A warning on every `reset` is ruled out; warn only when the exact command would intersect nonempty at-risk state. **Source classification:** new, justified by immediate loss of uncommitted content and by the high measured noise of the broad family. **Confidence:** confirmed for the broad reset count; inferred for the proposed path-impact predicate. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`] [INFERENCE: exact at-risk paths can be computed from pre-command index/worktree state, while reset flags are absent from reflog evidence]\n19: \n20: 6. **Deleting a local branch with unmerged commits — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch -D <branch>...` (not ordinary `-d`, whose merged check is already a Git safety gate). **Pre-execution state:** whether each named branch is checked out in any registered worktree, its configured upstream, and commits unique relative to upstream and the live integration tip. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; absence of a remote branch does not prove no other clone has the commits. **Noise:** not measured because branch deletion argv is not retained in HEAD reflog/history. **Source classification:** new with justification, derived from ALWAYS #17's required worktree-before-branch order and its proof-before-reap model. **Confidence:** inferred; the source confirms the ordering and safety model, not a current advisory. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330] [INFERENCE: `-D` bypasses the merged-branch protection that makes ordinary `-d` lower risk]\n21: \n22: 7. **Forced worktree removal — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git worktree remove --force <path>` or `git worktree remove -f <path>`. **Pre-execution state:** target registration, cleanliness from `git -C <path> status --porcelain`, branch merge status against the actual live integration tip, lock state, and the wrapper-session marker/PID when present. **Genuinely available:** yes if the target path and common Git directory remain readable; a missing/unreadable activity marker must remain unknown, not be interpreted as inactive. **Noise:** not measured; Git history does not retain worktree-removal commands. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule ALWAYS #17, which explicitly requires clean, merged, proven-inactive state for automatic removal and otherwise report-only behavior. **Confidence:** confirmed. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330]\n23: \n24: 8. **Force-updating or deleting remote branches — command plus local/remote state, with enforcement de-duplication.** **Operation:** `git push [<remote>] --force|--force-with-lease [<refspec>...]`, leading-`+` refspecs, `git push <remote> --delete <branch>...`, or deletion refspecs such as `:<dst>`. **Pre-execution state:** parsed destination refs, resolved remote URL, local expected old SHA/lease, local remote-tracking SHA, allowlist, and whether the destination is `main`/`master`/a protected integration branch. **Genuinely available:** local state yes; authoritative remote tip and server protection require a network read and can race after the check, so they are only partially available. **Noise:** not measured because push argv and rejected pushes do not enter reflogs. **Source classification:** named sk-git rules NEVER #1 and ESCALATE #4 for protected force-push; remote deletion is new with justification. The existing pre-push hook already enforces naming and permission for branch refs, so a new advisory should not duplicate those messages; it should target force/delete semantics the hook does not inspect. **Confidence:** confirmed for current hook coverage and gap; inferred for remote race handling. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-500] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-159]\n25: \n26: 9. **Rewriting or deleting release tags — command plus partially remote state.** **Operation:** `git tag -f <tag> [<object>]`, `git tag -d <tag>...`, `git push <remote> --force refs/tags/<tag>`, or `git push <remote> :refs/tags/<tag>`. **Pre-execution state:** whether the local tag exists, annotated/lightweight type, target object, version-like naming, and any locally known remote tag; authoritative remote existence requires `git ls-remote --tags` and can race. **Genuinely available:** local state yes, remote state only partially. **Noise:** not measured. **Source classification:** new with justification: rewriting a release identity is high cost, and current pre-push explicitly skips tags and other non-branch refs. **Confidence:** confirmed for the enforcement gap; inferred for a version-tag-only advisory threshold. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [INFERENCE: limiting the predicate to an existing version-like tag avoids warning on normal first-time tag creation]\n27: \n28: 10. **Destroying stash/recovery pointers — stateful advisory only when recovery state is implicated.** **Operation:** `git stash clear`, `git stash drop [<stash>]`, `git reflog expire ... refs/stash`, or an immediate-prune operation while stash/autostash recovery pointers exist. **Pre-execution state:** stash entries and messages, `MERGE_AUTOSTASH`/autostash refs, `refs/autostash-rescue/*`, unmerged index state, and whether the selected commit is reachable from another durable ref. **Genuinely available:** yes before deletion/pruning while the refs still exist. **Noise:** fire frequency not measured; this checkout currently has 20 stash entries, which demonstrates nonempty state but is not an invocation-rate denominator. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule ALWAYS #14 for autostash orphan risk, extended to the exact destructive follow-on commands. **Confidence:** confirmed for named risk and current stash count; inferred for reachability-based suppression. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git stash list`]\n29: \n30: 11. **Implicit broad staging by commit — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit -a|--all ...`. **Pre-execution state:** already-staged paths, modified/deleted tracked paths that `-a` would add, total path count, and top-level ownership spread. **Genuinely available:** yes for the resulting pre-hook index candidate, but whether a path belongs to another session is not encoded in Git and therefore cannot be confirmed. **Noise:** exact `-a` use was not measured; 1,090/1,482 reflog entries (73.55%) are commits, so generic commit warnings are categorically too noisy. **Source classification:** new extension of named scoped-staging discipline and the broad-add incident; it goes beyond `git add <dir>` because `commit -a` performs its own broad tracked-file staging. **Confidence:** confirmed for the available Git sets and broad commit rate; inferred for any ownership/top-level heuristic. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:215-248] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n31: \n32: 12. **Repository-wide history rewrite or immediate object pruning — command plus recovery-state advisory.** **Operation:** `git filter-branch ...`, `git filter-repo ...`, `git reflog expire --expire=now --all`, `git gc --prune=now`, or `git prune --expire=now`. **Pre-execution state:** clean/dirty status, current refs and worktrees, stash/recovery refs, remote-tracking refs, and objects reachable only through reflogs or rescue refs. **Genuinely available:** most local state is available before execution, although exhaustively proving external consumers or remote recoverability is impossible. **Noise:** not measured; these argv are not retained in normal Git history. **Source classification:** new with justification: these are explicit, rare operations capable of removing the recovery path for rewritten or orphaned commits, including the autostash-loss scenario named by ALWAYS #14. **Confidence:** inferred for advisory design; confirmed only that the named source treats stash drop/clear/GC as the permanent-loss boundary. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [INFERENCE: explicit immediate-expiry/prune tokens are quieter classifiers than warning on routine `git gc`]\n33: \n34: ## Command/State Boundary and Noise Threshold\n35: \n36: - **Command-only predicates reliable enough for the current evaluator:** direct branch-creation shapes and direct `--no-verify`; explicit destructive tokens such as `--hard`, `--force`, `-D`, `--prune=now`, and deletion refspecs are also command-classifiable, but should usually be combined with state to avoid noise. The checker already demonstrates exact-regex, fail-open command predicates, but has no repository-state argument. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:71-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:105-125]\n37: - **Stateful predicates genuinely available pre-execution:** current worktree/index/status, config, local refs, registered worktrees, local remote-tracking refs, remotes, and active Git-operation marker refs/files. Network-backed remote state is only a snapshot and is raceable. Operator intent, path ownership by another session, and what another clone has fetched are not Git state. [INFERENCE: based on the local-state fields required by Findings 3-12 and the pre-push hook's distinction between local input refs and remote SHA input at execution time]\n38: - **Provisional noise line:** unconditional advisories are defensible only for explicit opt-out or destructive-intent shapes that are expected to be rare. Any broad family at or above roughly 1% of observed Git transitions should be state-gated; the sample places amend at 0.47%, rebase at 2.97%, reset at 13.56%, and commit at 73.55%. A practical initial budget is **under 1 advisory per 100 Git mutations per rule and under 3 per 100 in aggregate**, but this is inferred, not validated: the repository lacks a Bash-hook invocation log, so reflog rates are prevalence ceilings rather than true advisory fire rates. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`] [INFERENCE: attention cost rises when generic rebase/reset/commit warnings repeatedly fire without confirmed hazardous state]\n39: \n40: ## Impossible Post-Execution-Only Checks\n41: \n42: 1. **Do not promise to predict merge/rebase/cherry-pick conflicts or successful autostash reapplication.** Pre-state can identify risk (dirty paths, merge bases, config), but the authoritative conflict/result state is created by execution and hooks. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327]\n43: 2. **Do not treat push success, HTTP 403, branch-protection acceptance, or authorization as preflight facts.** Active account/remote owner and local refs are clues; server policy, collaborator rights, concurrent remote updates, and network outcome are decided remotely. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:495-501]\n44: 3. **Do not claim the exact final commit tree from `git commit --only <paths>` before hooks run.** The current index/worktree and a dry-run can expose likely omissions, but pre-commit hooks can inspect or mutate state, and the actual commit object does not exist yet. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:46-118] [INFERENCE: post-hook commit contents are not identical to a guaranteed pre-hook snapshot]\n45: 4. **Do not infer actual command options from Git history.** Reflog records outcomes such as `reset`, `rebase`, and `commit (amend)`, not the complete argv, so it cannot distinguish `reset --hard` from safer reset modes or count `--no-verify`, `commit -a`, force-push, branch deletion, or worktree removal. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n46: \n47: ## Ruled Out\n48: \n49: - Unconditional warnings for every `git commit`, `git reset`, `git rebase`, `git pull`, or `git push`: measured broad-family rates and existing enforcement make these attention-consuming rather than protective.\n50: - A second advisory for non-allowlisted push permission or new-branch naming: the pre-push hook already blocks and gives an exact retry; only force/delete/tag semantics are uncovered.\n51: - Using `git log` or reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: neither preserves full argv or failed commands.\n52: - Treating a successful preflight remote query as authoritative through execution: remote state can change between check and push.\n53: \n54: ## Dead Ends\n55: \n56: - Exact historical fire rates for option-sensitive predicates cannot be recovered from ordinary Git history. The reducer should preserve this as negative knowledge; future noise calibration needs opt-in PreToolUse telemetry that records only normalized operation class and whether the state predicate matched, not raw commands.\n57: \n58: ## Edge Cases\n59: \n60: - Ambiguous input: the scope could include Git aliases and arbitrary scripts; this iteration chose direct shell-visible `git`/`git -C` invocations because they are the narrowest reliably classifiable surface. Alias/script expansion is deferred.\n61: - Contradictory evidence: none.\n62: - Missing dependencies: no Bash-hook invocation telemetry exists, so exact advisory noise was unavailable; bounded HEAD reflog and repository counts were used only as explicit upper bounds/proxies.\n63: - Partial success: none; the operation/state inventory was completed, while exact noise calibration remains a later question.\n64: \n65: ## Sources Consulted\n66: \n67: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-245,309-330,482-504`\n68: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:136-160,215-269`\n69: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96`\n70: - `.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:21-125`\n71: - `.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/SKILL.md:1-19` (frontmatter hard-rule schema example; its check IDs are not implemented by the inspected evaluator)\n72: - `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:1-165`\n73: - `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:1-118`\n74: - `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/commit-msg:1-186`\n75: - Bounded repository evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`, `git log --format=%s`, `git status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=all`, `git stash list`, `git worktree list --porcelain`, and `git for-each-ref` under local/remote refs.\n76: \n77: ## Assessment\n78: \n79: - New information ratio: 0.92 (10 findings fully new beyond the briefing's incidents; 2 partially new extensions)\n80: - Questions addressed: command-classifiable operation families; genuinely available pre-execution state; high-cost failures beyond the five incidents; preliminary noise boundary; operations that should remain silent due to enforcement or unavailable state.\n81: - Questions answered: which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution.\n82: \n83: ## Reflection\n84: \n85: - What worked and why: pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD-reflog sample separated command risk from alert frequency and exposed where existing enforcement already owns the outcome.\n86: - What did not work and why: Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it stores resulting ref actions, not shell argv.\n87: - What I would do differently: instrument a privacy-preserving dry-run classifier over future PreToolUse events, recording normalized family and predicate match only, to replace reflog proxies with actual fire-rate denominators.\n88: \n89: ## Recommended Next Focus\n90: \n91: Design and test bounded read-only state probes for the highest-value stateful candidates—published-HEAD amend/rebase, destructive path overwrite, forced worktree/branch deletion, and remote/tag force/delete—then estimate their actual match rate against sampled present repository states without implementing the hook.\n\n(End of file - total 91 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Iteration 1: Git Operation Families and Pre-Execution State\n\n## Focus\nInventory direct `git` invocation families that a Bash `PreToolUse` hook can classify before execution, then separate command-only predicates from checks that need repository, worktree, index, ref, remote, or config reads. The selected interpretation is limited to direct shell-visible Git shapes; aliases, shell functions, commands assembled by `eval`, and arbitrary scripts are deferred because classifying their eventual Git subprocess is not reliable from the original Bash text.\n\n## Findings\n\nThe current evaluator is a command-string engine: `evaluate(command, rules)` passes only the composed command to pure check functions. It can host command-only predicates today, but every stateful candidate below would require the PreToolUse path to add bounded read-only probes and a trustworthy command working directory. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:68-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:101-125]\n\n1. **Direct branch creation — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch <new> [<start>]`, `git checkout -b|-B <new> [<start>]`, or `git switch -c|-C <new> [<start>]` (including direct `git -C <repo> ...` forms). **Pre-execution state:** the command shape is sufficient for the named prohibition; branch existence and registered worktrees are optional refinements obtainable with `git show-ref --verify` and `git worktree list --porcelain`. **Genuinely available:** yes for a direct invocation; no for a Git command hidden in an opaque script or alias expansion. **Noise:** not measured—Git reflogs do not preserve these argv forms, and only 7/1,482 HEAD-reflog entries were checkouts, which is not a branch-creation count. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #2; it requires branch creation through the allocator-backed `git worktree add -b` flow. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule and command classifier; inferred for operational fire rate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-221] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-486] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n2. **Blanket hook bypass — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git commit ... --no-verify` or `git push ... --no-verify`, accepting option placement before or after the subcommand's ordinary arguments. **Pre-execution state:** only the parsed presence of `--no-verify`; repository state is not needed. **Genuinely available:** yes for direct commands. **Noise:** not measured because reflogs and commit history omit invocation options. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #9; this is especially advisory-worthy because the operation disables the enforcement layer that would otherwise own the check. **Confidence:** confirmed. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-493] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:46-55]\n\n3. **Amending published or integrated HEAD — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit --amend ...` (including `--amend --no-edit`). **Pre-execution state:** resolve `HEAD`; test whether a local remote-tracking ref or protected/integration branch contains that commit (`git for-each-ref --contains HEAD refs/remotes refs/heads`), and identify the current branch/worktree. **Genuinely available:** locally yes, but only relative to possibly stale remote-tracking refs; whether another clone has consumed the commit is not knowable pre-execution. **Noise:** 7 amend events among 1,482 current-worktree HEAD-reflog entries (0.47%); the hazardous published subset was not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #10. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule, available local state, and repository count; inferred for publication completeness. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:492-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n4. **Rebasing published/shared history — stateful advisory, not a generic rebase warning.** **Operation:** `git rebase ...`, `git pull --rebase ...`, or `git pull ...` when `pull.rebase=true`; include an autostash dimension when `--autostash`, `rebase.autoStash=true`, or `merge.autoStash=true` applies. **Pre-execution state:** current branch/detachment, remote-tracking refs containing the commits to rewrite, ahead/behind counts, dirty-path count, unmerged entries, and relevant Git config. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; remote freshness and concurrent consumers remain unknown. **Noise:** 44 rebase events among 1,482 HEAD-reflog entries (2.97%); an unconditional rebase warning would therefore be materially noisier than a published-history-only predicate. The exact autostash subset was not measured because reflogs omit flags/config. **Source classification:** named sk-git rules NEVER #8 and ALWAYS #14; it extends, rather than restates, the briefing incident by gating on publication state as well as dirty-tree/autostash risk. **Confidence:** confirmed for local state and measured broad rate; inferred for the final combined predicate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:491-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n5. **Discarding worktree/index content — stateful advisory across four exact shapes.** **Operation:** `git reset --hard [<tree-ish>]`, `git clean -f|-fd|-fdx [-- <pathspec>...]`, `git checkout -- <pathspec>...`, or `git restore --worktree [--source=<tree-ish>] [-- <pathspec>...]`. **Pre-execution state:** pathspec-resolved tracked modifications, staged changes that `reset --hard` would replace, matching untracked/ignored paths for `clean`, and unmerged entries. **Genuinely available:** yes via read-only diffs/status plus Git's own pathspec semantics before execution; exact shell-expanded paths must be parsed first. **Noise:** the reflog contains 201 reset events (13.56%), but does not record reset mode, so 13.56% is only a broad upper bound; `clean`, path checkout, and restore frequency were not measured. A warning on every `reset` is ruled out; warn only when the exact command would intersect nonempty at-risk state. **Source classification:** new, justified by immediate loss of uncommitted content and by the high measured noise of the broad family. **Confidence:** confirmed for the broad reset count; inferred for the proposed path-impact predicate. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`] [INFERENCE: exact at-risk paths can be computed from pre-command index/worktree state, while reset flags are absent from reflog evidence]\n\n6. **Deleting a local branch with unmerged commits — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch -D <branch>...` (not ordinary `-d`, whose merged check is already a Git safety gate). **Pre-execution state:** whether each named branch is checked out in any registered worktree, its configured upstream, and commits unique relative to upstream and the live integration tip. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; absence of a remote branch does not prove no other clone has the commits. **Noise:** not measured because branch deletion argv is not retained in HEAD reflog/history. **Source classification:** new with justification, derived from ALWAYS #17's required worktree-before-branch order and its proof-before-reap model. **Confidence:** inferred; the source confirms the ordering and safety model, not a current advisory. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330] [INFERENCE: `-D` bypasses the merged-branch protection that makes ordinary `-d` lower risk]","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-001.md","text":"# Iteration 1: Git Operation Families and Pre-Execution State\n\n## Focus\nInventory direct `git` invocation families that a Bash `PreToolUse` hook can classify before execution, then separate command-only predicates from checks that need repository, worktree, index, ref, remote, or config reads. The selected interpretation is limited to direct shell-visible Git shapes; aliases, shell functions, commands assembled by `eval`, and arbitrary scripts are deferred because classifying their eventual Git subprocess is not reliable from the original Bash text.\n\n## Findings\n\nThe current evaluator is a command-string engine: `evaluate(command, rules)` passes only the composed command to pure check functions. It can host command-only predicates today, but every stateful candidate below would require the PreToolUse path to add bounded read-only probes and a trustworthy command working directory. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:68-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:101-125]\n\n1. **Direct branch creation — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch <new> [<start>]`, `git checkout -b|-B <new> [<start>]`, or `git switch -c|-C <new> [<start>]` (including direct `git -C <repo> ...` forms). **Pre-execution state:** the command shape is sufficient for the named prohibition; branch existence and registered worktrees are optional refinements obtainable with `git show-ref --verify` and `git worktree list --porcelain`. **Genuinely available:** yes for a direct invocation; no for a Git command hidden in an opaque script or alias expansion. **Noise:** not measured—Git reflogs do not preserve these argv forms, and only 7/1,482 HEAD-reflog entries were checkouts, which is not a branch-creation count. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #2; it requires branch creation through the allocator-backed `git worktree add -b` flow. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule and command classifier; inferred for operational fire rate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-221] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-486] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n2. **Blanket hook bypass — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git commit ... --no-verify` or `git push ... --no-verify`, accepting option placement before or after the subcommand's ordinary arguments. **Pre-execution state:** only the parsed presence of `--no-verify`; repository state is not needed. **Genuinely available:** yes for direct commands. **Noise:** not measured because reflogs and commit history omit invocation options. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #9; this is especially advisory-worthy because the operation disables the enforcement layer that would otherwise own the check. **Confidence:** confirmed. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-493] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:46-55]\n\n3. **Amending published or integrated HEAD — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit --amend ...` (including `--amend --no-edit`). **Pre-execution state:** resolve `HEAD`; test whether a local remote-tracking ref or protected/integration branch contains that commit (`git for-each-ref --contains HEAD refs/remotes refs/heads`), and identify the current branch/worktree. **Genuinely available:** locally yes, but only relative to possibly stale remote-tracking refs; whether another clone has consumed the commit is not knowable pre-execution. **Noise:** 7 amend events among 1,482 current-worktree HEAD-reflog entries (0.47%); the hazardous published subset was not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #10. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule, available local state, and repository count; inferred for publication completeness. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:492-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n4. **Rebasing published/shared history — stateful advisory, not a generic rebase warning.** **Operation:** `git rebase ...`, `git pull --rebase ...`, or `git pull ...` when `pull.rebase=true`; include an autostash dimension when `--autostash`, `rebase.autoStash=true`, or `merge.autoStash=true` applies. **Pre-execution state:** current branch/detachment, remote-tracking refs containing the commits to rewrite, ahead/behind counts, dirty-path count, unmerged entries, and relevant Git config. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; remote freshness and concurrent consumers remain unknown. **Noise:** 44 rebase events among 1,482 HEAD-reflog entries (2.97%); an unconditional rebase warning would therefore be materially noisier than a published-history-only predicate. The exact autostash subset was not measured because reflogs omit flags/config. **Source classification:** named sk-git rules NEVER #8 and ALWAYS #14; it extends, rather than restates, the briefing incident by gating on publication state as well as dirty-tree/autostash risk. **Confidence:** confirmed for local state and measured broad rate; inferred for the final combined predicate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:491-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n5. **Discarding worktree/index content — stateful advisory across four exact shapes.** **Operation:** `git reset --hard [<tree-ish>]`, `git clean -f|-fd|-fdx [-- <pathspec>...]`, `git checkout -- <pathspec>...`, or `git restore --worktree [--source=<tree-ish>] [-- <pathspec>...]`. **Pre-execution state:** pathspec-resolved tracked modifications, staged changes that `reset --hard` would replace, matching untracked/ignored paths for `clean`, and unmerged entries. **Genuinely available:** yes via read-only diffs/status plus Git's own pathspec semantics before execution; exact shell-expanded paths must be parsed first. **Noise:** the reflog contains 201 reset events (13.56%), but does not record reset mode, so 13.56% is only a broad upper bound; `clean`, path checkout, and restore frequency were not measured. A warning on every `reset` is ruled out; warn only when the exact command would intersect nonempty at-risk state. **Source classification:** new, justified by immediate loss of uncommitted content and by the high measured noise of the broad family. **Confidence:** confirmed for the broad reset count; inferred for the proposed path-impact predicate. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`] [INFERENCE: exact at-risk paths can be computed from pre-command index/worktree state, while reset flags are absent from reflog evidence]\n\n6. **Deleting a local branch with unmerged commits — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch -D <branch>...` (not ordinary `-d`, whose merged check is already a Git safety gate). **Pre-execution state:** whether each named branch is checked out in any registered worktree, its configured upstream, and commits unique relative to upstream and the live integration tip. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; absence of a remote branch does not prove no other clone has the commits. **Noise:** not measured because branch deletion argv is not retained in HEAD reflog/history. **Source classification:** new with justification, derived from ALWAYS #17's required worktree-before-branch order and its proof-before-reap model. **Confidence:** inferred; the source confirms the ordering and safety model, not a current advisory. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330] [INFERENCE: `-D` bypasses the merged-branch protection that makes ordinary `-d` lower risk]\n\n7. **Forced worktree removal — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git worktree remove --force <path>` or `git worktree remove -f <path>`. **Pre-execution state:** target registration, cleanliness from `git -C <path> status --porcelain`, branch merge status against the actual live integration tip, lock state, and the wrapper-session marker/PID when present. **Genuinely available:** yes if the target path and common Git directory remain readable; a missing/unreadable activity marker must remain unknown, not be interpreted as inactive. **Noise:** not measured; Git history does not retain worktree-removal commands. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule ALWAYS #17, which explicitly requires clean, merged, proven-inactive state for automatic removal and otherwise report-only behavior. **Confidence:** confirmed. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330]\n\n8. **Force-updating or deleting remote branches — command plus local/remote state, with enforcement de-duplication.** **Operation:** `git push [<remote>] --force|--force-with-lease [<refspec>...]`, leading-`+` refspecs, `git push <remote> --delete <branch>...`, or deletion refspecs such as `:<dst>`. **Pre-execution state:** parsed destination refs, resolved remote URL, local expected old SHA/lease, local remote-tracking SHA, allowlist, and whether the destination is `main`/`master`/a protected integration branch. **Genuinely available:** local state yes; authoritative remote tip and server protection require a network read and can race after the check, so they are only partially available. **Noise:** not measured because push argv and rejected pushes do not enter reflogs. **Source classification:** named sk-git rules NEVER #1 and ESCALATE #4 for protected force-push; remote deletion is new with justification. The existing pre-push hook already enforces naming and permission for branch refs, so a new advisory should not duplicate those messages; it should target force/delete semantics the hook does not inspect. **Confidence:** confirmed for current hook coverage and gap; inferred for remote race handling. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-500] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-159]\n\n9. **Rewriting or deleting release tags — command plus partially remote state.** **Operation:** `git tag -f <tag> [<object>]`, `git tag -d <tag>...`, `git push <remote> --force refs/tags/<tag>`, or `git push <remote> :refs/tags/<tag>`. **Pre-execution state:** whether the local tag exists, annotated/lightweight type, target object, version-like naming, and any locally known remote tag; authoritative remote existence requires `git ls-remote --tags` and can race. **Genuinely available:** local state yes, remote state only partially. **Noise:** not measured. **Source classification:** new with justification: rewriting a release identity is high cost, and current pre-push explicitly skips tags and other non-branch refs. **Confidence:** confirmed for the enforcement gap; inferred for a version-tag-only advisory threshold. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [INFERENCE: limiting the predicate to an existing version-like tag avoids warning on normal first-time tag creation]\n\n10. **Destroying stash/recovery pointers — stateful advisory only when recovery state is implicated.** **Operation:** `git stash clear`, `git stash drop [<stash>]`, `git reflog expire ... refs/stash`, or an immediate-prune operation while stash/autostash recovery pointers exist. **Pre-execution state:** stash entries and messages, `MERGE_AUTOSTASH`/autostash refs, `refs/autostash-rescue/*`, unmerged index state, and whether the selected commit is reachable from another durable ref. **Genuinely available:** yes before deletion/pruning while the refs still exist. **Noise:** fire frequency not measured; this checkout currently has 20 stash entries, which demonstrates nonempty state but is not an invocation-rate denominator. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule ALWAYS #14 for autostash orphan risk, extended to the exact destructive follow-on commands. **Confidence:** confirmed for named risk and current stash count; inferred for reachability-based suppression. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git stash list`]\n\n11. **Implicit broad staging by commit — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit -a|--all ...`. **Pre-execution state:** already-staged paths, modified/deleted tracked paths that `-a` would add, total path count, and top-level ownership spread. **Genuinely available:** yes for the resulting pre-hook index candidate, but whether a path belongs to another session is not encoded in Git and therefore cannot be confirmed. **Noise:** exact `-a` use was not measured; 1,090/1,482 reflog entries (73.55%) are commits, so generic commit warnings are categorically too noisy. **Source classification:** new extension of named scoped-staging discipline and the broad-add incident; it goes beyond `git add <dir>` because `commit -a` performs its own broad tracked-file staging. **Confidence:** confirmed for the available Git sets and broad commit rate; inferred for any ownership/top-level heuristic. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:215-248] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n12. **Repository-wide history rewrite or immediate object pruning — command plus recovery-state advisory.** **Operation:** `git filter-branch ...`, `git filter-repo ...`, `git reflog expire --expire=now --all`, `git gc --prune=now`, or `git prune --expire=now`. **Pre-execution state:** clean/dirty status, current refs and worktrees, stash/recovery refs, remote-tracking refs, and objects reachable only through reflogs or rescue refs. **Genuinely available:** most local state is available before execution, although exhaustively proving external consumers or remote recoverability is impossible. **Noise:** not measured; these argv are not retained in normal Git history. **Source classification:** new with justification: these are explicit, rare operations capable of removing the recovery path for rewritten or orphaned commits, including the autostash-loss scenario named by ALWAYS #14. **Confidence:** inferred for advisory design; confirmed only that the named source treats stash drop/clear/GC as the permanent-loss boundary. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [INFERENCE: explicit immediate-expiry/prune tokens are quieter classifiers than warning on routine `git gc`]\n\n## Command/State Boundary and Noise Threshold\n\n- **Command-only predicates reliable enough for the current evaluator:** direct branch-creation shapes and direct `--no-verify`; explicit destructive tokens such as `--hard`, `--force`, `-D`, `--prune=now`, and deletion refspecs are also command-classifiable, but should usually be combined with state to avoid noise. The checker already demonstrates exact-regex, fail-open command predicates, but has no repository-state argument. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:71-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:105-125]\n- **Stateful predicates genuinely available pre-execution:** current worktree/index/status, config, local refs, registered worktrees, local remote-tracking refs, remotes, and active Git-operation marker refs/files. Network-backed remote state is only a snapshot and is raceable. Operator intent, path ownership by another session, and what another clone has fetched are not Git state. [INFERENCE: based on the local-state fields required by Findings 3-12 and the pre-push hook's distinction between local input refs and remote SHA input at execution time]\n- **Provisional noise line:** unconditional advisories are defensible only for explicit opt-out or destructive-intent shapes that are expected to be rare. Any broad family at or above roughly 1% of observed Git transitions should be state-gated; the sample places amend at 0.47%, rebase at 2.97%, reset at 13.56%, and commit at 73.55%. A practical initial budget is **under 1 advisory per 100 Git mutations per rule and under 3 per 100 in aggregate**, but this is inferred, not validated: the repository lacks a Bash-hook invocation log, so reflog rates are prevalence ceilings rather than true advisory fire rates. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`] [INFERENCE: attention cost rises when generic rebase/reset/commit warnings repeatedly fire without confirmed hazardous state]\n\n## Impossible Post-Execution-Only Checks\n\n1. **Do not promise to predict merge/rebase/cherry-pick conflicts or successful autostash reapplication.** Pre-state can identify risk (dirty paths, merge bases, config), but the authoritative conflict/result state is created by execution and hooks. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327]\n2. **Do not treat push success, HTTP 403, branch-protection acceptance, or authorization as preflight facts.** Active account/remote owner and local refs are clues; server policy, collaborator rights, concurrent remote updates, and network outcome are decided remotely. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:495-501]\n3. **Do not claim the exact final commit tree from `git commit --only <paths>` before hooks run.** The current index/worktree and a dry-run can expose likely omissions, but pre-commit hooks can inspect or mutate state, and the actual commit object does not exist yet. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:46-118] [INFERENCE: post-hook commit contents are not identical to a guaranteed pre-hook snapshot]\n4. **Do not infer actual command options from Git history.** Reflog records outcomes such as `reset`, `rebase`, and `commit (amend)`, not the complete argv, so it cannot distinguish `reset --hard` from safer reset modes or count `--no-verify`, `commit -a`, force-push, branch deletion, or worktree removal. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Unconditional warnings for every `git commit`, `git reset`, `git rebase`, `git pull`, or `git push`: measured broad-family rates and existing enforcement make these attention-consuming rather than protective.\n- A second advisory for non-allowlisted push permission or new-branch naming: the pre-push hook already blocks and gives an exact retry; only force/delete/tag semantics are uncovered.\n- Using `git log` or reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: neither preserves full argv or failed commands.\n- Treating a successful preflight remote query as authoritative through execution: remote state can change between check and push.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Exact historical fire rates for option-sensitive predicates cannot be recovered from ordinary Git history. The reducer should preserve this as negative knowledge; future noise calibration needs opt-in PreToolUse telemetry that records only normalized operation class and whether the state predicate matched, not raw commands.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: the scope could include Git aliases and arbitrary scripts; this iteration chose direct shell-visible `git`/`git -C` invocations because they are the narrowest reliably classifiable surface. Alias/script expansion is deferred.\n- Contradictory evidence: none.\n- Missing dependencies: no Bash-hook invocation telemetry exists, so exact advisory noise was unavailable; bounded HEAD reflog and repository counts were used only as explicit upper bounds/proxies.\n- Partial success: none; the operation/state inventory was completed, while exact noise calibration remains a later question.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-245,309-330,482-504`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:136-160,215-269`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96`\n- `.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:21-125`\n- `.opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/SKILL.md:1-19` (frontmatter hard-rule schema example; its check IDs are not implemented by the inspected evaluator)\n- `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:1-165`\n- `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:1-118`\n- `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/commit-msg:1-186`\n- Bounded repository evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`, `git log --format=%s`, `git status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=all`, `git stash list`, `git worktree list --porcelain`, and `git for-each-ref` under local/remote refs.\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.92 (10 findings fully new beyond the briefing's incidents; 2 partially new extensions)\n- Questions addressed: command-classifiable operation families; genuinely available pre-execution state; high-cost failures beyond the five incidents; preliminary noise boundary; operations that should remain silent due to enforcement or unavailable state.\n- Questions answered: which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD-reflog sample separated command risk from alert frequency and exposed where existing enforcement already owns the outcome.\n- What did not work and why: Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it stores resulting ref actions, not shell argv.\n- What I would do differently: instrument a privacy-preserving dry-run classifier over future PreToolUse events, recording normalized family and predicate match only, to replace reflog proxies with actual fire-rate denominators.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nDesign and test bounded read-only state probes for the highest-value stateful candidates—published-HEAD amend/rebase, destructive path overwrite, forced worktree/branch deletion, and remote/tag force/delete—then estimate their actual match rate against sampled present repository states without implementing the hook.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":91,"totalLines":91,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-001.md","time":{"start":1785179091070,"end":1785179091081}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0513eee204a8c91a016a67abd2e8ec819199655ca3acd56f92"}},"id":"prt_fa4f730380018cI4hsqjP3tM5S","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa4f70c3f001os0vM15SqKAiqm"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785179091090,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_iOc9FITAOpWw8A0uHut5zYr2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-001.jsonl"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-001.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":1,\"run\":1,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Inventory command-classifiable git operation families and map each to genuinely available pre-execution state\",\"findingsCount\":12,\"newInfoRatio\":0.92,\"noveltyJustification\":\"10 of 12 findings were fully new beyond the briefing incidents and 2 were partially new extensions of broad-staging and autostash risks.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\",\"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\"],\"answeredQuestions\":[\"Which direct git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\"],\"ruledOut\":[\"Unconditional advisories for generic commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push families\",\"Duplicating existing pre-push branch naming and permission enforcement\",\"Using git log or reflog as exact argv telemetry\",\"Treating raceable remote snapshots or post-execution outcomes as preflight facts\"],\"toolsUsed\":[\"Skill\",\"Read\",\"Glob\",\"Bash\"],\"sourcesQueried\":[\".opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md\",\".opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md\",\".opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md\",\".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs\",\".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/SKILL.md\",\".opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push\",\".opencode/scripts/git-hooks/p... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(End of file - total 1 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":1,\"run\":1,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Inventory command-classifiable git operation families and map each to genuinely available pre-execution state\",\"findingsCount\":12,\"newInfoRatio\":0.92,\"noveltyJustification\":\"10 of 12 findings were fully new beyond the briefing incidents and 2 were partially new extensions of broad-staging and autostash risks.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\",\"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\"],\"answeredQuestions\":[\"Which direct git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\"],\"ruledOut\":[\"Unconditional advisories for generic commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push families\",\"Duplicating existing pre-push branch naming and permission enforcement\",\"Using git log or reflog as exact argv telemetry\",\"Treating raceable remote snapshots or post-execution outcomes as preflight facts\"],\"toolsUsed\":[\"Skill\",\"Read\",\"Glob\",\"Bash\"],\"sourcesQueried\":[\".opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md\",\".opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md\",\".opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md\",\".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs\",\".opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-devin/SKILL.md\",\".opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push\",\".opencode/scripts/git-hooks/p... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-001.jsonl","text":"{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":1,\"run\":1,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Inventory command-classifiable git operation families and map each to genuinely available pre-execution state\",\"findingsCount\":12,\"newInfoRatio\":0.92,\"noveltyJustification\":\"10 of 12 findings were fully new beyond the briefing incidents and 2 were partially new extensions of broad-staging and autostash risks.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\",\"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely av