{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785179127896,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_wU71sj2COlOmWVtdJHtiBL5p","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n@@\n-- [ ] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n+- [x] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n@@\n ## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n-[None yet]\n+- Which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution?\n@@\n ## 7. WHAT WORKED\n-[None yet]\n+- Pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD reflog separated command risk from broad-family prevalence. (iteration 1)\n@@\n ## 8. WHAT FAILED\n-[None yet]\n+- Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it records ref outcomes rather than complete argv. (iteration 1)\n@@\n ## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n-[None yet]\n+- Generic warnings for every commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push: broad-family prevalence and existing enforcement make them noisy. (iteration 1)\n+- Treating Git log/reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: flags, pathspecs, and failed commands are absent. (iteration 1)\n@@\n ## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\n-[None yet]\n+- Duplicate advisories for pre-push naming and non-allowlisted push permission already owned by blocking enforcement. (iteration 1)\n+- Treating remote snapshots, push authorization, conflict outcomes, or post-hook commit contents as guaranteed pre-execution facts. (iteration 1)\n@@\n ## 11A. CARRIED-FORWARD OPEN QUESTIONS\n-[None yet]\n+- Which bounded state probes make published amend/rebase, destructive overwrite, branch/worktree deletion, and remote/tag force/delete predicates precise enough? (iteration 1)\n+- How often do state-gated candidates match, given that ordinary Git history cannot provide exact argv telemetry? (iteration 1)\n@@\n ## 11. NEXT FOCUS\n-Inventory command-classifiable git operation families and map each to pre-execution state that can actually be read before execution.\n+Investigate destructive history and recovery operations: published amend/rebase, hard reset/clean/restore, branch/worktree deletion, stash/reflog destruction, immediate pruning, and the bounded read-only state probes that distinguish real risk from routine use.\n*** Update File: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n@@\n-  \"keyQuestions\": [],\n-  \"openQuestions\": [],\n-  \"resolvedQuestions\": [],\n-  \"carriedForwardOpenQuestions\": [],\n-  \"uncoveredQuestions\": [],\n-  \"keyFindings\": [],\n-  \"ruledOutDirections\": [],\n+  \"keyQuestions\": [\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"q-operation-families\",\n+      \"text\": \"Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\",\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 1\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"q-prestate\",\n+      \"text\": \"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\n+      \"resolved\": false\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"q-new-rules\",\n+      \"text\": \"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\n+      \"resolved\": false\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"q-noise\",\n+      \"text\": \"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\n+      \"resolved\": false\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"q-silence\",\n+      \"text\": \"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable or too noisy?\",\n+      \"resolved\": false\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \"openQuestions\": [\"q-prestate\", \"q-new-rules\", \"q-noise\", \"q-silence\"],\n+  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\"q-operation-families\"],\n+  \"carriedForwardOpenQuestions\": [\n+    \"Which bounded state probes make destructive-history predicates precise enough?\",\n+    \"How often do state-gated candidates match without exact argv telemetry?\"\n+  ],\n+  \"uncoveredQuestions\": [],\n+  \"keyFindings\": [\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f1-command-state-boundary\",\n+      \"iteration\": 1,\n+      \"text\": \"The evaluator accepts only command text today; direct branch creation and --no-verify are command-only, while most valuable destructive candidates need bounded repository-state probes.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f1-noise-proxy\",\n+      \"iteration\": 1,\n+      \"text\": \"Broad reflog prevalence was amend 0.47%, rebase 2.97%, reset 13.56%, and commit 73.55%; broad family warnings above roughly 1% require state gating.\"\n+    }\n+  ],\n+  \"ruledOutDirections\": [\n+    \"Unconditional warnings for generic commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push\",\n+    \"Duplicating existing pre-push branch naming and permission enforcement\",\n+    \"Using Git history as exact argv telemetry\",\n+    \"Treating raceable remote snapshots or post-execution outcomes as facts\"\n+  ],\n@@\n-    \"iterationsCompleted\": 0,\n-    \"openQuestions\": 5,\n-    \"resolvedQuestions\": 0,\n-    \"keyFindings\": 0,\n-    \"convergenceScore\": 0,\n-    \"coverageBySources\": {}\n+    \"iterationsCompleted\": 1,\n+    \"openQuestions\": 4,\n+    \"resolvedQuestions\": 1,\n+    \"keyFindings\": 12,\n+    \"convergenceScore\": 0.92,\n+    \"coverageBySources\": {\"code\": 11, \"history\": 3}\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\nM .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n+++ /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n@@ -4,9 +4,9 @@\n Determine which git invocation shapes justify a one-line pre-execution advisory, which repository and command state is genuinely available before execution, and which candidate rules remain quiet enough to preserve operator attention.\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n ## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\n-- [ ] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n+- [x] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n - [ ] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n - [ ] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n - [ ] How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\n - [ ] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n@@ -25,30 +25,32 @@\n - Preserve eliminated high-noise and non-pre-evaluable alternatives as primary negative knowledge.\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n ## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n-[None yet]\n+- Which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution?\n <!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n \n <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\n <!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n ## 7. WHAT WORKED\n-[None yet]\n+- Pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD reflog separated command risk from broad-family prevalence. (iteration 1)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n ## 8. WHAT FAILED\n-[None yet]\n+- Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it records ref outcomes rather than complete argv. (iteration 1)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n ## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n-[None yet]\n+- Generic warnings for every commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push: broad-family prevalence and existing enforcement make them noisy. (iteration 1)\n+- Treating Git log/reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: flags, pathspecs, and failed commands are absent. (iteration 1)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n ## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\n-[None yet]\n+- Duplicate advisories for pre-push naming and non-allowlisted push permission already owned by blocking enforcement. (iteration 1)\n+- Treating remote snapshots, push authorization, conflict outcomes, or post-hook commit contents as guaranteed pre-execution facts. (iteration 1)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n ## 10A. SATURATED DIRECTIONS AND DIVERGENCE FRONTIER\n@@ -61,14 +63,15 @@\n <!-- /ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n ## 11A. CARRIED-FORWARD OPEN QUESTIONS\n-[None yet]\n+- Which bounded state probes make published amend/rebase, destructive overwrite, branch/worktree deletion, and remote/tag force/delete predicates precise enough? (iteration 1)\n+- How often do state-gated candidates match, given th