{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1785180939932,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"id":"prt_fa5136697001uluCPEVuEf06DJ","messageID":"msg_fa5135a2700191hmT9Lb3ogp5G","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","snapshot":"fe03b20dde9c4dbba7dc0e6f79ecce2c3c838fcc","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785180943027,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_xlrKxutGas6R2Wu0VKGGka95","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-004.md"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-004.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Iteration 4: Worktree, Remote, and Account Coordination\n2: \n3: ## Canonical Route Proof\n4: \n5: `Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research`; `agent_definition_loaded=true`; detached lineage `sol`; run/iteration `4`; LEAF execution with no sub-agent dispatch.\n6: \n7: ## Focus\n8: \n9: Investigate direct push operations whose local worktree, effective destination, autosync environment, GitHub account, destructive refspec, or multi-ref shape creates a useful pre-execution coordination fact. The narrow boundary is a direct shell-visible `git`/`git -C` or `gh` command. Positive local facts can justify a neutral advisory; authentication, server policy, push acceptance, and the state remaining unchanged through execution cannot be promised.\n10: \n11: ## Findings\n12: \n13: 1. **Detached/linked-worktree reconciliation is predictable as a local-ref consequence, not as push success.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> HEAD:<branch>` (or another source ref targeting `<branch>`) while the current Git directory is detached or belongs to a linked worktree and another registered worktree has local `<branch>` checked out. **Specific pre-execution state:** parse the effective destination; compare absolute `--git-dir` with `--git-common-dir`; read `git worktree list --porcelain`; resolve the source SHA, the other worktree's HEAD, cleanliness, and ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for the local snapshot. A successful remote push will not itself move the other local branch/worktree, but push success and whether a follower advances that tree concurrently remain unknown. **Noise:** exact invocation frequency is unmeasured; current-state exposure is measured at 35 worktrees, 1 detached, and 6 worktree tips not contained in the primary checkout's current HEAD. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #15 plus the observed detached-push incident. **Confidence:** confirmed for Git-directory/worktree state, current counts, and local-ref non-movement; inferred for whether the operator would otherwise miss the work. Retain one advisory only when the exact destination is checked out elsewhere and the source is detached/linked; do not warn on every push. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-330] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git worktree list --porcelain`; per-tip `merge-base --is-ancestor`]\n14: \n15: 2. **Push rules must resolve the effective destination before classifying coordination risk.** **Operation:** argument-less `git push`, `git push <remote>` without refspecs, explicit `<src>:<dst>`, and `git -C <repo> push ...`; include `branch.<name>.pushRemote`, upstream, `remote.<name>.push`, `push.default`, push URLs, and URL rewrites. **Specific pre-execution state:** actual cwd/repository, current branch or detachment, branch upstream, all push-specific config, remote/push URL, and parsed refspecs. The current checkout is on `skilled/v4.0.0.0` with matching `origin` upstream and `push.autoSetupRemote=true`, but several other local branches are configured to merge differently named or shared release refs, proving that local branch text is not a universal destination proxy. **Genuinely available before execution:** local configuration and explicit mappings are available; ambiguous Git expansion can be reproduced only by faithfully implementing Git's precedence or using a dry run that may contact the remote and invoke hooks. **Noise:** exact push-shape frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new prerequisite justified by destination-sensitive advisories. **Confidence:** confirmed for documented precedence and current config; inferred for the cheapest complete resolver. Remain silent rather than guessing when destination expansion is ambiguous. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git config --get-regexp` for remote, branch, and push keys]\n16: \n17: 3. **A manual live-branch push from a wrapper session bypasses the safer autosync primitive and merits a narrow advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git push origin HEAD:<live>` from a linked `work/<runtime>/<slug>` session when `SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1` and `<live> == $SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH`; exclude `bash .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh`, whose nested push is not the direct PreToolUse command. **Specific pre-execution state:** linked-worktree identity, current branch, wrapper namespace, the two environment variables, destination branch, tracked cleanliness, and source/live ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for a direct command and environment snapshot. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured; current session is `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` in the primary checkout with both autosync variables unset, so this predicate would not fire now. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #16, extended to command classification. **Confidence:** confirmed. `git-sync.sh` fetches, retries push races, rebases only a clean tracked tree, and aborts conflicts; the hand-rolled push skips that coordination. The pre-push permission exemption is not evidence that manual publication is safe—it exists so the scripted primitive can publish without blocking. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-329] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/continuous-integration.md:78-103] [SOURCE: bounded environment evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n18: \n19: 4. **Active `gh` account versus remote owner is positive identity evidence, not a Git-push authorization oracle.** **Operation:** `gh pr create`, `gh release create`, and—with lower confidence—`git push` to a GitHub HTTPS remote. **Specific pre-execution state:** resolve the effective push URL and owner; query the active host account (`gh api user`) and repository permission snapshot; separately inspect which credential helper/transport Git will use. Current evidence is positive: remote owner `MichelKerkmeester`, active account `MichelKerkmeester`, and GitHub reports `push=true`/`admin=true` for that authenticated API viewer. **Genuinely available before execution:** identity and API permission are network snapshots. They are directly relevant to `gh` commands; they do not prove which credential Git's HTTPS/SSH transport will present, branch-protection bypass, server-hook acceptance, or future validity. **Noise:** mismatch frequency is unmeasured; the predicate is false in the current checkout. **Source classification:** observed 403 incident, refined beyond a string mismatch. **Confidence:** confirmed for current identity/permission and the limitation; inferred for a generic Git credential relationship. Advise on a positive owner/account mismatch for `gh`; for `git push`, require transport/credential evidence or phrase it only as “account differs,” never “push will 403.” [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:21-32] [SOURCE: bounded network evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `gh api user`; `gh api repos/MichelKerkmeester/opencode--skilled-agent-loops-with-spec-kit-memory`]\n20: \n21: 5. **Force-with-lease forms have materially different preflight strength.** **Operation:** `git push ... --force`, `-f`, leading-`+` refspecs, `--force-with-lease`, `--force-with-lease=<ref>`, and `--force-with-lease=<ref>:<expect>`; include `--force-if-includes`. **Specific pre-execution state:** exact destinations, local source SHA, explicit expected SHA when present, local remote-tracking tips, background-fetch risk, and whether multiple refs are selected. **Genuinely available before execution:** command tokens and local refs are available. Only the explicit `<ref>:<expect>` form fixes the intended expected value in the command; acceptance is still an execution-time server comparison. Bare lease forms use remote-tracking refs and can be weakened by background fetch, while `--force` applies to every selected ref. **Noise:** option-specific use is unmeasured; ordinary history cannot recover push argv or rejected pushes. **Source classification:** named NEVER #1 / ESCALATE #4, refined with official lease semantics. **Confidence:** confirmed. Recommend an advisory for unconditional force, leading `+`, or a broad lease without explicit expectation when the destination would retreat/rewrite known history; lower or suppress it for explicit leases with no positive rewrite evidence. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-500] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`]\n22: \n23: 6. **Remote deletion has quiet exact shapes and broad high-cost variants.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> --delete <ref>...`, empty-source refspecs `:<dst>`, `--prune` with a mapping, and `--mirror` (which also force-updates and deletes refs). **Specific pre-execution state:** resolved destination namespaces; positive local remote-tracking/tag snapshots; destination checked-out/integration status; ref count selected by prune/mirror; and allowlist/hook coverage. **Genuinely available before execution:** local mappings and known refs are available, but the authoritative remote set is a raceable network snapshot and server deletion policy is unknown. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new beyond the briefing, justified by irreversible shared-ref removal and the batch reach of prune/mirror. **Confidence:** confirmed for command semantics; inferred for remote impact from stale local refs. Retain exact delete/mirror/prune advisories only when at least one known destination exists; never claim the deletion will be accepted. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`] [INFERENCE: positive local destination evidence is sufficient to describe intent but not authoritative remote outcome]\n24: \n25: 7. **Tag force/delete operations are outside both current pre-push gates.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> --force refs/tags/<tag>`, `+refs/tags/<tag>:refs/tags/<tag>`, `git push <remote> :refs/tags/<tag>`, `--delete <tag>` when it resolves to a tag, and force combined with `--tags`. **Specific pre-execution state:** destination namespace, local tag existence/type/target, version-like naming, known remote-tracking/tag snapshot, and whether the source changes an existing target. The checkout currently has 143 local tags. **Genuinely available before execution:** local tag facts are available; authoritative remote target and release consumers are not. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured; 143 tags measure exposure, not fire rate. **Source classification:** new enforcement-gap refinement. **Confidence:** confirmed. The hook's `refs/heads/*` case skips every tag; a fabricated tag-deletion input returned 0. Advise only on force/delete/mirror semantics with positive known tag evidence, not ordinary first-time tag publication. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [SOURCE: bounded hook matrix and tag inventory captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n26: \n27: 8. **Multi-ref and multi-remote destructive pushes can partially land unless atomicity is explicitly and successfully negotiated.** **Operation:** one push selecting multiple refs through multiple refspecs, `--all`, `--tags`, `--mirror`, or `--prune`; and a configured remote group that expands into sequential independent pushes. **Specific pre-execution state:** expanded destination count, destructive/force members, presence of `--atomic`, remote-group membership, and local remote capability knowledge. **Genuinely available before execution:** command/config expansion is locally available; server support for atomic push and per-remote outcomes are execution-time facts. Official Git behavior makes remote-group members independent and rejects `--atomic` for a group. **Noise:** exact multi-ref/group invocation frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new high-cost coordination rule beyond the five incidents. **Confidence:** confirmed for semantics; inferred for expected prevalence. Advise only when multiple selected destinations include force/delete/mirror/prune or when a remote group makes partial cross-remote publication possible; generic multi-ref publication would be noisy. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [INFERENCE: destructive multi-destination selection creates materially higher partial-outcome cost than an ordinary single-ref push]\n28: \n29: 9. **Permission/naming advisories duplicate execution-time blocking logic and should be silent—but current installation is contradictory evidence, not permission to duplicate.** **Operation:** creating/updating a non-allowlisted `refs/heads/*` on `origin`, or creating a malformed/wrapper branch. **Specific pre-execution state:** destination branch, new/update status, allowlist, bypass variables, autosync exact-live exception, and installed-hook presence. The versioned hook blocks a fabricated conformant nonallowlisted branch, allows `skilled/v*`, allows only the exact autosync live branch, and blocks a different branch. However, `core.hooksPath` currently resolves to `.git/hooks` and `.git/hooks/pre-push` is absent. **Genuinely available before execution:** all local inputs and hook installation are available; the real pre-push hook's remote SHA arrives later at push execution. **Noise:** exact blocked-push frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** named remote-push policy and current-state contradiction. **Confidence:** confirmed. The advisory layer should stay silent for the same naming/permission messages because the blocking hook owns them and prints the retry; `--no-verify` remains separately advisory-worthy because it deliberately bypasses that owner. A missing installation belongs to the existing SessionStart hook-health warning, not a repeated per-push imitation. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-163] [SOURCE: bounded installed-hook and fabricated-stdin evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n30: \n31: 10. **The exact preflight boundary is local positive evidence; authorization and final remote outcomes remain raceable.** **Operation:** every retained push advisory above. **Specific pre-execution state:** direct command text, cwd/`-C`, effective local config, current refs, worktree registry, environment, hook installation, and optionally an authenticated remote/API snapshot. **Genuinely available before execution:** local snapshots are genuinely available; shell-generated arguments, concurrent ref/worktree changes, credentials actually selected by transport, server hooks/protection, force-lease comparison, atomic capability, and push result are not final until execution. **Noise:** push argv/failure telemetry is absent, so all option-specific historical fire rates are explicitly unmeasured; measured current facts (35 worktrees, 6 tips outside primary HEAD, 143 tags, matching owner/account, missing installed pre-push) are exposure measurements only. **Source classification:** synthesis of local source, current state, and official remote semantics. **Confidence:** confirmed for the boundary; inferred for eventual attention cost. One advisory should coalesce all positive facts per command, and absent/mismatched snapshots must never be translated into “safe,” “authorized,” “will fail,” or “will succeed.” [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [INFERENCE: a pre-execution snapshot cannot bind concurrent local writers or the remote receive transaction]\n32: \n33: ## Advisory/Enforcement Boundary\n34: \n35: - **Keep silent because the blocking hook already owns it:** new-branch owner grammar, wrapper-branch remote publication, and fresh permission for each non-allowlisted `origin` branch push. Do not spend a second line restating the same block/retry. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:95-159]\n36: - **Retain because the hook does not own it:** detached/linked destination checked out elsewhere; manual wrapper push that bypasses `git-sync.sh`; unconditional/broad force semantics on allowlisted branches; branch deletion of allowlisted refs; tag force/delete; destructive multi-ref or multi-remote partial-outcome risk; and positive `gh` owner/account mismatch. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155]\n37: - **Current installation conflict:** the tracked hook exists but the configured installed path lacks `pre-push`. This weakens today's technical backstop without changing advisory ownership. The correct remedy is hook-health/install workflow, not duplicating an exact blocker inside a nonblocking advisory. [SOURCE: bounded installed-hook evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n38: \n39: ## Ruled Out\n40: \n41: - Generic warnings for every push, every detached HEAD, every linked worktree, every account/owner comparison, or every multi-ref push.\n42: - Claims that matching `gh` identity proves Git transport identity or that API `push=true` proves branch-protection/server-hook acceptance.\n43: - A duplicate one-line advisory for non-allowlisted branch permission or new-branch naming already owned by pre-push.\n44: - Treating a successful `ls-remote`/API snapshot, local remote-tracking SHA, or preflight force lease as authoritative through remote execution.\n45: - Warnings for ordinary first-time tag publication or normal non-destructive multi-ref pushes.\n46: \n47: ## Dead Ends\n48: \n49: - Ordinary Git history still cannot recover force/delete/tag options, failed/blocked pushes, remote-group partial outcomes, or exact advisory matches. This approach remains exhausted; no additional reflog pass can produce push-argv noise rates.\n50: - A network preflight cannot eliminate the race: it adds latency and yields another snapshot, while server authorization, protection, hooks, and leases are decided during receive.\n51: \n52: ## Edge Cases\n53: \n54: - Ambiguous input: “active account” could mean `gh` API identity, HTTPS credential-helper identity, or SSH key identity. This iteration treats `gh` identity as authoritative only for `gh` commands and requires transport evidence before relating it to `git push`.\n55: - Contradictory evidence: policy and briefing assign blocking ownership to pre-push, but the current configured hook path has no installed `pre-push`. Both facts are preserved; the missing install is a hook-health problem, not evidence for duplicating blockers in advisories.\n56: - Missing dependencies: normalized PreToolUse/push-attempt telemetry is absent, so option-specific historical fire rates remain unmeasured.\n57: - Partial success: no remote mutation was permitted, so actual push authorization/protection outcomes were intentionally not tested; current authenticated repository permission was gathered read-only and labeled as a snapshot.\n58: \n59: ## Sources Consulted\n60: \n61: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-245,327-340,482-504`\n62: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96`\n63: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372,511-514`\n64: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/worktree-workflows.md:23-64,137-201`\n65: - `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/continuous-integration.md:36-103,142-147`\n66: - `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:1-165`\n67: - `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/post-commit:35-48`\n68: - `.opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh:140-175,189-201,322-333`\n69: - `.opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:39-83,90-155`\n70: - `https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push`\n71: - Installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`\n72: - Bounded current evidence: repository/worktree/remotes/config/account/environment/ref inventory, GitHub repository permission API, installed-hook check, fabricated pre-push stdin matrix, and worktree-tip containment. No fetch, push, ref mutation, staging, commit, or checkout was performed.\n73: \n74: ## Assessment\n75: \n76: - New information ratio: 0.75 (5 of 10 findings fully new; 5 partially new refinements; `(5 + 0.5*5)/10 = 0.75`)\n77: - Questions addressed: genuinely available coordination/account/remote pre-state; high-cost push cases beyond the incidents; state-gated noise; exact advisories that should remain silent because blocking enforcement owns them.\n78: - Questions answered: which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify preflight advisories despite race windows; which branch naming/permission advisories duplicate blocking hooks and should stay silent.\n79: \n80: ## Reflection\n81: \n82: - What worked and why: resolving exact current worktree, remote, environment, account, permission, and hook-installation state against both the scripts and official push semantics separated deterministic local consequences from authorization guesses.\n83: - What did not work and why: ordinary history has no push argv/failure record, and a remote read cannot close the receive-time race; operation-specific fire rates therefore remain unmeasured.\n84: - What I would do differently: capture normalized future telemetry for destination namespace/count, operation class, worktree class, positive state predicate, existing-hook ownership, and emitted/coalesced advisory—without raw URLs, branch names, paths, or credentials.\n85: \n86: ## Recommended Next Focus\n87: \n88: Use iteration 5 to calibrate the final candidate matrix and aggregate noise budget: combine retained rules from iterations 1-4, rank by positive-evidence strength and overlap, define one-line coalescing/latency limits, and preserve every unavailable/raceable outcome as explicit negative knowledge.\n\n(End of file - total 88 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Iteration 4: Worktree, Remote, and Account Coordination\n\n## Canonical Route Proof\n\n`Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research`; `agent_definition_loaded=true`; detached lineage `sol`; run/iteration `4`; LEAF execution with no sub-agent dispatch.\n\n## Focus\n\nInvestigate direct push operations whose local worktree, effective destination, autosync environment, GitHub account, destructive refspec, or multi-ref shape creates a useful pre-execution coordination fact. The narrow boundary is a direct shell-visible `git`/`git -C` or `gh` command. Positive local facts can justify a neutral advisory; authentication, server policy, push acceptance, and the state remaining unchanged through execution cannot be promised.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **Detached/linked-worktree reconciliation is predictable as a local-ref consequence, not as push success.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> HEAD:<branch>` (or another source ref targeting `<branch>`) while the current Git directory is detached or belongs to a linked worktree and another registered worktree has local `<branch>` checked out. **Specific pre-execution state:** parse the effective destination; compare absolute `--git-dir` with `--git-common-dir`; read `git worktree list --porcelain`; resolve the source SHA, the other worktree's HEAD, cleanliness, and ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for the local snapshot. A successful remote push will not itself move the other local branch/worktree, but push success and whether a follower advances that tree concurrently remain unknown. **Noise:** exact invocation frequency is unmeasured; current-state exposure is measured at 35 worktrees, 1 detached, and 6 worktree tips not contained in the primary checkout's current HEAD. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #15 plus the observed detached-push incident. **Confidence:** confirmed for Git-directory/worktree state, current counts, and local-ref non-movement; inferred for whether the operator would otherwise miss the work. Retain one advisory only when the exact destination is checked out elsewhere and the source is detached/linked; do not warn on every push. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-330] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git worktree list --porcelain`; per-tip `merge-base --is-ancestor`]\n\n2. **Push rules must resolve the effective destination before classifying coordination risk.** **Operation:** argument-less `git push`, `git push <remote>` without refspecs, explicit `<src>:<dst>`, and `git -C <repo> push ...`; include `branch.<name>.pushRemote`, upstream, `remote.<name>.push`, `push.default`, push URLs, and URL rewrites. **Specific pre-execution state:** actual cwd/repository, current branch or detachment, branch upstream, all push-specific config, remote/push URL, and parsed refspecs. The current checkout is on `skilled/v4.0.0.0` with matching `origin` upstream and `push.autoSetupRemote=true`, but several other local branches are configured to merge differently named or shared release refs, proving that local branch text is not a universal destination proxy. **Genuinely available before execution:** local configuration and explicit mappings are available; ambiguous Git expansion can be reproduced only by faithfully implementing Git's precedence or using a dry run that may contact the remote and invoke hooks. **Noise:** exact push-shape frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new prerequisite justified by destination-sensitive advisories. **Confidence:** confirmed for documented precedence and current config; inferred for the cheapest complete resolver. Remain silent rather than guessing when destination expansion is ambiguous. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git config --get-regexp` for remote, branch, and push keys]\n\n3. **A manual live-branch push from a wrapper session bypasses the safer autosync primitive and merits a narrow advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git push origin HEAD:<live>` from a linked `work/<runtime>/<slug>` session when `SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1` and `<live> == $SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH`; exclude `bash .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh`, whose nested push is not the direct PreToolUse command. **Specific pre-execution state:** linked-worktree identity, current branch, wrapper namespace, the two environment variables, destination branch, tracked cleanliness, and source/live ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for a direct command and environment snapshot. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured; current session is `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` in the primary checkout with both autosync variables unset, so this predicate would not fire now. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #16, extended to command classification. **Confidence:** confirmed. `git-sync.sh` fetches, retries push races, rebases only a clean tracked tree, and aborts conflicts; the hand-rolled push skips that coordination. The pre-push permission exemption is not evidence that manual publication is safe—it exists so the scripted primitive can publish without blocking. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-329] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/continuous-integration.md:78-103] [SOURCE: bounded environment evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n4. **Active `gh` account versus remote owner is positive identity evidence, not a Git-push authorization oracle.** **Operation:** `gh pr create`, `gh release create`, and—with lower confidence—`git push` to a GitHub HTTPS remote. **Specific pre-execution state:** resolve the effective push URL and owner; query the active host account (`gh api user`) and repository permission snapshot; separately inspect which credential helper/transport Git will use. Current evidence is positive: remote owner `MichelKerkmeester`, active account `MichelKerkmeester`, and GitHub reports `push=true`/`admin=true` for that authenticated API viewer. **Genuinely available before execution:** identity and API permission are network snapshots. They are directly relevant to `gh` commands; they do not prove which credential Git's HTTPS/SSH transport will present, branch-protection bypass, server-hook acceptance, or future validity. **Noise:** mismatch frequency is unmeasured; the predicate is false in the current checkout. **Source classification:** observed 403 incident, refined beyond a string mismatch. **Confidence:** confirmed for current identity/permission and the limitation; inferred for a generic Git credential relationship. Advise on a positive owner/account mismatch for `gh`; for `git push`, require transport/credential evidence or phrase it only as “account differs,” never “push will 403.” [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:21-32] [SOURCE: bounded network evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `gh api user`; `gh api repos/MichelKerkmeester/opencode--skilled-agent-loops-with-spec-kit-memory`]\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-004.md","text":"# Iteration 4: Worktree, Remote, and Account Coordination\n\n## Canonical Route Proof\n\n`Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research`; `agent_definition_loaded=true`; detached lineage `sol`; run/iteration `4`; LEAF execution with no sub-agent dispatch.\n\n## Focus\n\nInvestigate direct push operations whose local worktree, effective destination, autosync environment, GitHub account, destructive refspec, or multi-ref shape creates a useful pre-execution coordination fact. The narrow boundary is a direct shell-visible `git`/`git -C` or `gh` command. Positive local facts can justify a neutral advisory; authentication, server policy, push acceptance, and the state remaining unchanged through execution cannot be promised.\n\n## Findings\n\n1. **Detached/linked-worktree reconciliation is predictable as a local-ref consequence, not as push success.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> HEAD:<branch>` (or another source ref targeting `<branch>`) while the current Git directory is detached or belongs to a linked worktree and another registered worktree has local `<branch>` checked out. **Specific pre-execution state:** parse the effective destination; compare absolute `--git-dir` with `--git-common-dir`; read `git worktree list --porcelain`; resolve the source SHA, the other worktree's HEAD, cleanliness, and ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for the local snapshot. A successful remote push will not itself move the other local branch/worktree, but push success and whether a follower advances that tree concurrently remain unknown. **Noise:** exact invocation frequency is unmeasured; current-state exposure is measured at 35 worktrees, 1 detached, and 6 worktree tips not contained in the primary checkout's current HEAD. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #15 plus the observed detached-push incident. **Confidence:** confirmed for Git-directory/worktree state, current counts, and local-ref non-movement; inferred for whether the operator would otherwise miss the work. Retain one advisory only when the exact destination is checked out elsewhere and the source is detached/linked; do not warn on every push. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-330] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git worktree list --porcelain`; per-tip `merge-base --is-ancestor`]\n\n2. **Push rules must resolve the effective destination before classifying coordination risk.** **Operation:** argument-less `git push`, `git push <remote>` without refspecs, explicit `<src>:<dst>`, and `git -C <repo> push ...`; include `branch.<name>.pushRemote`, upstream, `remote.<name>.push`, `push.default`, push URLs, and URL rewrites. **Specific pre-execution state:** actual cwd/repository, current branch or detachment, branch upstream, all push-specific config, remote/push URL, and parsed refspecs. The current checkout is on `skilled/v4.0.0.0` with matching `origin` upstream and `push.autoSetupRemote=true`, but several other local branches are configured to merge differently named or shared release refs, proving that local branch text is not a universal destination proxy. **Genuinely available before execution:** local configuration and explicit mappings are available; ambiguous Git expansion can be reproduced only by faithfully implementing Git's precedence or using a dry run that may contact the remote and invoke hooks. **Noise:** exact push-shape frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new prerequisite justified by destination-sensitive advisories. **Confidence:** confirmed for documented precedence and current config; inferred for the cheapest complete resolver. Remain silent rather than guessing when destination expansion is ambiguous. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git config --get-regexp` for remote, branch, and push keys]\n\n3. **A manual live-branch push from a wrapper session bypasses the safer autosync primitive and merits a narrow advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git push origin HEAD:<live>` from a linked `work/<runtime>/<slug>` session when `SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1` and `<live> == $SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH`; exclude `bash .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh`, whose nested push is not the direct PreToolUse command. **Specific pre-execution state:** linked-worktree identity, current branch, wrapper namespace, the two environment variables, destination branch, tracked cleanliness, and source/live ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for a direct command and environment snapshot. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured; current session is `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` in the primary checkout with both autosync variables unset, so this predicate would not fire now. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #16, extended to command classification. **Confidence:** confirmed. `git-sync.sh` fetches, retries push races, rebases only a clean tracked tree, and aborts conflicts; the hand-rolled push skips that coordination. The pre-push permission exemption is not evidence that manual publication is safe—it exists so the scripted primitive can publish without blocking. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-329] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/continuous-integration.md:78-103] [SOURCE: bounded environment evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n4. **Active `gh` account versus remote owner is positive identity evidence, not a Git-push authorization oracle.** **Operation:** `gh pr create`, `gh release create`, and—with lower confidence—`git push` to a GitHub HTTPS remote. **Specific pre-execution state:** resolve the effective push URL and owner; query the active host account (`gh api user`) and repository permission snapshot; separately inspect which credential helper/transport Git will use. Current evidence is positive: remote owner `MichelKerkmeester`, active account `MichelKerkmeester`, and GitHub reports `push=true`/`admin=true` for that authenticated API viewer. **Genuinely available before execution:** identity and API permission are network snapshots. They are directly relevant to `gh` commands; they do not prove which credential Git's HTTPS/SSH transport will present, branch-protection bypass, server-hook acceptance, or future validity. **Noise:** mismatch frequency is unmeasured; the predicate is false in the current checkout. **Source classification:** observed 403 incident, refined beyond a string mismatch. **Confidence:** confirmed for current identity/permission and the limitation; inferred for a generic Git credential relationship. Advise on a positive owner/account mismatch for `gh`; for `git push`, require transport/credential evidence or phrase it only as “account differs,” never “push will 403.” [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:21-32] [SOURCE: bounded network evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `gh api user`; `gh api repos/MichelKerkmeester/opencode--skilled-agent-loops-with-spec-kit-memory`]\n\n5. **Force-with-lease forms have materially different preflight strength.** **Operation:** `git push ... --force`, `-f`, leading-`+` refspecs, `--force-with-lease`, `--force-with-lease=<ref>`, and `--force-with-lease=<ref>:<expect>`; include `--force-if-includes`. **Specific pre-execution state:** exact destinations, local source SHA, explicit expected SHA when present, local remote-tracking tips, background-fetch risk, and whether multiple refs are selected. **Genuinely available before execution:** command tokens and local refs are available. Only the explicit `<ref>:<expect>` form fixes the intended expected value in the command; acceptance is still an execution-time server comparison. Bare lease forms use remote-tracking refs and can be weakened by background fetch, while `--force` applies to every selected ref. **Noise:** option-specific use is unmeasured; ordinary history cannot recover push argv or rejected pushes. **Source classification:** named NEVER #1 / ESCALATE #4, refined with official lease semantics. **Confidence:** confirmed. Recommend an advisory for unconditional force, leading `+`, or a broad lease without explicit expectation when the destination would retreat/rewrite known history; lower or suppress it for explicit leases with no positive rewrite evidence. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-500] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`]\n\n6. **Remote deletion has quiet exact shapes and broad high-cost variants.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> --delete <ref>...`, empty-source refspecs `:<dst>`, `--prune` with a mapping, and `--mirror` (which also force-updates and deletes refs). **Specific pre-execution state:** resolved destination namespaces; positive local remote-tracking/tag snapshots; destination checked-out/integration status; ref count selected by prune/mirror; and allowlist/hook coverage. **Genuinely available before execution:** local mappings and known refs are available, but the authoritative remote set is a raceable network snapshot and server deletion policy is unknown. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new beyond the briefing, justified by irreversible shared-ref removal and the batch reach of prune/mirror. **Confidence:** confirmed for command semantics; inferred for remote impact from stale local refs. Retain exact delete/mirror/prune advisories only when at least one known destination exists; never claim the deletion will be accepted. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`] [INFERENCE: positive local destination evidence is sufficient to describe intent but not authoritative remote outcome]\n\n7. **Tag force/delete operations are outside both current pre-push gates.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> --force refs/tags/<tag>`, `+refs/tags/<tag>:refs/tags/<tag>`, `git push <remote> :refs/tags/<tag>`, `--delete <tag>` when it resolves to a tag, and force combined with `--tags`. **Specific pre-execution state:** destination namespace, local tag existence/type/target, version-like naming, known remote-tracking/tag snapshot, and whether the source changes an existing target. The checkout currently has 143 local tags. **Genuinely available before execution:** local tag facts are available; authoritative remote target and release consumers are not. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured; 143 tags measure exposure, not fire rate. **Source classification:** new enforcement-gap refinement. **Confidence:** confirmed. The hook's `refs/heads/*` case skips every tag; a fabricated tag-deletion input returned 0. Advise only on force/delete/mirror semantics with positive known tag evidence, not ordinary first-time tag publication. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [SOURCE: bounded hook matrix and tag inventory captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n8. **Multi-ref and multi-remote destructive pushes can partially land unless atomicity is explicitly and successfully negotiated.** **Operation:** one push selecting multiple refs through multiple refspecs, `--all`, `--tags`, `--mirror`, or `--prune`; and a configured remote group that expands into sequential independent pushes. **Specific pre-execution state:** expanded destination count, destructive/force members, presence of `--atomic`, remote-group membership, and local remote capability knowledge. **Genuinely available before execution:** command/config expansion is locally available; server support for atomic push and per-remote outcomes are execution-time facts. Official Git behavior makes remote-group members independent and rejects `--atomic` for a group. **Noise:** exact multi-ref/group invocation frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new high-cost coordination rule beyond the five incidents. **Confidence:** confirmed for semantics; inferred for expected prevalence. Advise only when multiple selected destinations include force/delete/mirror/prune or when a remote group makes partial cross-remote publication possible; generic multi-ref publication would be noisy. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [INFERENCE: destructive multi-destination selection creates materially higher partial-outcome cost than an ordinary single-ref push]\n\n9. **Permission/naming advisories duplicate execution-time blocking logic and should be silent—but current installation is contradictory evidence, not permission to duplicate.** **Operation:** creating/updating a non-allowlisted `refs/heads/*` on `origin`, or creating a malformed/wrapper branch. **Specific pre-execution state:** destination branch, new/update status, allowlist, bypass variables, autosync exact-live exception, and installed-hook presence. The versioned hook blocks a fabricated conformant nonallowlisted branch, allows `skilled/v*`, allows only the exact autosync live branch, and blocks a different branch. However, `core.hooksPath` currently resolves to `.git/hooks` and `.git/hooks/pre-push` is absent. **Genuinely available before execution:** all local inputs and hook installation are available; the real pre-push hook's remote SHA arrives later at push execution. **Noise:** exact blocked-push frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** named remote-push policy and current-state contradiction. **Confidence:** confirmed. The advisory layer should stay silent for the same naming/permission messages because the blocking hook owns them and prints the retry; `--no-verify` remains separately advisory-worthy because it deliberately bypasses that owner. A missing installation belongs to the existing SessionStart hook-health warning, not a repeated per-push imitation. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-163] [SOURCE: bounded installed-hook and fabricated-stdin evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n10. **The exact preflight boundary is local positive evidence; authorization and final remote outcomes remain raceable.** **Operation:** every retained push advisory above. **Specific pre-execution state:** direct command text, cwd/`-C`, effective local config, current refs, worktree registry, environment, hook installation, and optionally an authenticated remote/API snapshot. **Genuinely available before execution:** local snapshots are genuinely available; shell-generated arguments, concurrent ref/worktree changes, credentials actually selected by transport, server hooks/protection, force-lease comparison, atomic capability, and push result are not final until execution. **Noise:** push argv/failure telemetry is absent, so all option-specific historical fire rates are explicitly unmeasured; measured current facts (35 worktrees, 6 tips outside primary HEAD, 143 tags, matching owner/account, missing installed pre-push) are exposure measurements only. **Source classification:** synthesis of local source, current state, and official remote semantics. **Confidence:** confirmed for the boundary; inferred for eventual attention cost. One advisory should coalesce all positive facts per command, and absent/mismatched snapshots must never be translated into “safe,” “authorized,” “will fail,” or “will succeed.” [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [INFERENCE: a pre-execution snapshot cannot bind concurrent local writers or the remote receive transaction]\n\n## Advisory/Enforcement Boundary\n\n- **Keep silent because the blocking hook already owns it:** new-branch owner grammar, wrapper-branch remote publication, and fresh permission for each non-allowlisted `origin` branch push. Do not spend a second line restating the same block/retry. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:95-159]\n- **Retain because the hook does not own it:** detached/linked destination checked out elsewhere; manual wrapper push that bypasses `git-sync.sh`; unconditional/broad force semantics on allowlisted branches; branch deletion of allowlisted refs; tag force/delete; destructive multi-ref or multi-remote partial-outcome risk; and positive `gh` owner/account mismatch. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155]\n- **Current installation conflict:** the tracked hook exists but the configured installed path lacks `pre-push`. This weakens today's technical backstop without changing advisory ownership. The correct remedy is hook-health/install workflow, not duplicating an exact blocker inside a nonblocking advisory. [SOURCE: bounded installed-hook evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n## Ruled Out\n\n- Generic warnings for every push, every detached HEAD, every linked worktree, every account/owner comparison, or every multi-ref push.\n- Claims that matching `gh` identity proves Git transport identity or that API `push=true` proves branch-protection/server-hook acceptance.\n- A duplicate one-line advisory for non-allowlisted branch permission or new-branch naming already owned by pre-push.\n- Treating a successful `ls-remote`/API snapshot, local remote-tracking SHA, or preflight force lease as authoritative through remote execution.\n- Warnings for ordinary first-time tag publication or normal non-destructive multi-ref pushes.\n\n## Dead Ends\n\n- Ordinary Git history still cannot recover force/delete/tag options, failed/blocked pushes, remote-group partial outcomes, or exact advisory matches. This approach remains exhausted; no additional reflog pass can produce push-argv noise rates.\n- A network preflight cannot eliminate the race: it adds latency and yields another snapshot, while server authorization, protection, hooks, and leases are decided during receive.\n\n## Edge Cases\n\n- Ambiguous input: “active account” could mean `gh` API identity, HTTPS credential-helper identity, or SSH key identity. This iteration treats `gh` identity as authoritative only for `gh` commands and requires transport evidence before relating it to `git push`.\n- Contradictory evidence: policy and briefing assign blocking ownership to pre-push, but the current configured hook path has no installed `pre-push`. Both facts are preserved; the missing install is a hook-health problem, not evidence for duplicating blockers in advisories.\n- Missing dependencies: normalized PreToolUse/push-attempt telemetry is absent, so option-specific historical fire rates remain unmeasured.\n- Partial success: no remote mutation was permitted, so actual push authorization/protection outcomes were intentionally not tested; current authenticated repository permission was gathered read-only and labeled as a snapshot.\n\n## Sources Consulted\n\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-245,327-340,482-504`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372,511-514`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/worktree-workflows.md:23-64,137-201`\n- `.opencode/skills/sk-git/references/continuous-integration.md:36-103,142-147`\n- `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:1-165`\n- `.opencode/scripts/git-hooks/post-commit:35-48`\n- `.opencode/bin/worktree-session.sh:140-175,189-201,322-333`\n- `.opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:39-83,90-155`\n- `https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push`\n- Installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`\n- Bounded current evidence: repository/worktree/remotes/config/account/environment/ref inventory, GitHub repository permission API, installed-hook check, fabricated pre-push stdin matrix, and worktree-tip containment. No fetch, push, ref mutation, staging, commit, or checkout was performed.\n\n## Assessment\n\n- New information ratio: 0.75 (5 of 10 findings fully new; 5 partially new refinements; `(5 + 0.5*5)/10 = 0.75`)\n- Questions addressed: genuinely available coordination/account/remote pre-state; high-cost push cases beyond the incidents; state-gated noise; exact advisories that should remain silent because blocking enforcement owns them.\n- Questions answered: which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify preflight advisories despite race windows; which branch naming/permission advisories duplicate blocking hooks and should stay silent.\n\n## Reflection\n\n- What worked and why: resolving exact current worktree, remote, environment, account, permission, and hook-installation state against both the scripts and official push semantics separated deterministic local consequences from authorization guesses.\n- What did not work and why: ordinary history has no push argv/failure record, and a remote read cannot close the receive-time race; operation-specific fire rates therefore remain unmeasured.\n- What I would do differently: capture normalized future telemetry for destination namespace/count, operation class, worktree class, positive state predicate, existing-hook ownership, and emitted/coalesced advisory—without raw URLs, branch names, paths, or credentials.\n\n## Recommended Next Focus\n\nUse iteration 5 to calibrate the final candidate matrix and aggregate noise budget: combine retained rules from iterations 1-4, rank by positive-evidence strength and overlap, define one-line coalescing/latency limits, and preserve every unavailable/raceable outcome as explicit negative knowledge.","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":88,"totalLines":88,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-004.md","time":{"start":1785180943021,"end":1785180943025}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0a01f7d4c8fd6ccc016a67b30ef16c8191a79a616fe33d8d1c"}},"id":"prt_fa51372780017putGtWhRpvleC","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa5135a2700191hmT9Lb3ogp5G"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785180943070,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_LKA0xxdo2srmLD3KiaTeRzio","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-004.jsonl"},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-004.jsonl</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: {\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":4,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"run\":4,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Investigate worktree, remote, and account coordination and exact preflight race/noise boundaries\",\"findingsCount\":10,\"newInfoRatio\":0.75,\"noveltyJustification\":\"5 of 10 findings were fully new and 5 were partially new refinements, yielding (5 + 0.5*5)/10 = 0.75.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable or too noisy?\"],\"answeredQuestions\":[\"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify preflight advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\",\"Which branch naming and permission advisories duplicate blocking hooks and should remain silent?\"],\"ruledOut\":[\"Generic warnings for every push, detached HEAD, linked worktree, account comparison, or multi-ref push\",\"Treating gh identity or API push permission as proof of Git transport identity or server acceptance\",\"Duplicating pre-push naming and non-allowlisted permission blockers\",\"Treating network or remote-tracking snapshots as authoritative through execution\",\"Warnings for ordinary first-time tag publication or nondestructive multi-ref pushes\"],\"focusTrack\":\"worktree-remote-account-coordination\",\"edgeCase\":\"contradictory-evidence\",\"toolsUsed\":[\"Skill\",\"Read\",\"Bash\",\"WebFetch\",\"ApplyPatch\"],\"sourcesQueried\":[\".opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-245,327-340,482-504\",\".opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96\",\".opencode/skills/sk-g... (line truncated to 2000 chars)\n\n(End of file - total 1 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":4,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"run\":4,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Investigate worktree, remote, and account coordination and exact preflight race/noise boundaries\",\"findingsCount\":10,\"newInfoRatio\":0.75,\"noveltyJustification\":\"5 of 10 findings were fully new and 5 were partially new refinements, yielding (5 + 0.5*5)/10 = 0.75.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable or too noisy?\"],\"answeredQuestions\":[\"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify preflight advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\",\"Which branch naming and permission advisories duplicate blocking hooks and should remain silent?\"],\"ruledOut\":[\"Generic warnings for every push, detached HEAD, linked worktree, account comparison, or multi-ref push\",\"Treating gh identity or API push permission as proof of Git transport identity or server acceptance\",\"Duplicating pre-push naming and non-allowlisted permission blockers\",\"Treating network or remote-tracking snapshots as authoritative through execution\",\"Warnings for ordinary first-time tag publication or nondestructive multi-ref pushes\"],\"focusTrack\":\"worktree-remote-account-coordination\",\"edgeCase\":\"contradictory-evidence\",\"toolsUsed\":[\"Skill\",\"Read\",\"Bash\",\"WebFetch\",\"ApplyPatch\"],\"sourcesQueried\":[\".opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-245,327-340,482-504\",\".opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96\",\".opencode/skills/sk-g... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-004.jsonl","text":"{\"type\":\"iteration\",\"iteration\":4,\"mode\":\"research\",\"target_agent\":\"deep-research\",\"agent_definition_loaded\":true,\"resolved_route\":\"Resolved route: mode=research target_agent=deep-research\",\"run\":4,\"status\":\"complete\",\"focus\":\"Investigate worktree, remote, and account coordination and exact preflight race/noise boundaries\",\"findingsCount\":10,\"newInfoRatio\":0.75,\"noveltyJustification\":\"5 of 10 findings were fully new and 5 were partially new refinements, yielding (5 + 0.5*5)/10 = 0.75.\",\"keyQuestions\":[\"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable or too noisy?\"],\"answeredQuestions\":[\"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify preflight advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\",\"Which branch naming and permission advisories duplicate blocking hooks and should remain silent?\"],\"ruledOut\":[\"Generic warnings for every push, detached HEAD, linked worktree, account comparison, or multi-ref push\",\"Treating gh identity or API push permission as proof of Git transport identity or server acceptance\",\"Duplicating pre-push naming and non-allowlisted permission blockers\",\"Treating network or remote-tracking snapshots as authoritative through execution\",\"Warnings for ordinary first-time tag publication or nondestructive multi-ref pushes\"],\"focusTrack\":\"worktree-remote-account-coordination\",\"edgeCase\":\"contradictory-evidence\",\"toolsUsed\":[\"Skill\",\"Read\",\"Bash\",\"WebFetch\",\"ApplyPatch\"],\"sourcesQueried\":[\".opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-245,327-340,482-504\",\".opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96\",\".opencode/skills/sk-g... (line truncated to 2000 chars)","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":1,"totalLines":1,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deltas/iter-004.jsonl","time":{"start":1785180943061,"end":1785180943069}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_0a01f7d4c8fd6ccc016a67b30ef1808191b39d231cec7d8a2d"}},"id":"prt_fa51372ce001UoNmOwELi8ol5I","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa5135a2700191hmT9Lb3ogp5G"}}
