{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1785180953379,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"id":"prt_fa5139b20001KgOFf7ukK5nxl2","messageID":"msg_fa513840f001EPco5PX0oJ5GN5","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","snapshot":"fe03b20dde9c4dbba7dc0e6f79ecce2c3c838fcc","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785180986232,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"apply_patch","callID":"call_8vYX2UzvIDHBG6ByBXCQ651y","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"patchText":"*** Begin Patch\n*** Update File: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n@@\n-- [ ] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n-- [ ] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n+- [x] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n+- [x] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n@@\n-- [ ] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n+- [x] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n@@\n ## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n - Which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution?\n+- Which local repository, worktree, index, config, ref, account, and remote snapshots are available, and which outcomes remain unknowable or raceable?\n+- Which high-cost destructive, staging, refspec, tag, and coordination failures beyond the briefing warrant candidate advisories?\n+- Which routine, recovery, enforced, ambiguous, or inherently post-execution cases should remain silent?\n@@\n - Combining official selection semantics, native dry runs, current hooks, and 500-commit path breadth produced objective mismatch predicates without inventing session ownership. (iteration 3)\n+- Resolving actual worktree, destination, account, environment, hook, and ref state separated deterministic local push consequences from authorization guesses. (iteration 4)\n@@\n - Ordinary history cannot recover add/commit options or hook-time snapshots, and Git state has no session-owner field. (iteration 3)\n+- Push history has no argv/failure record, and network reads cannot close receive-time races; current missing hook installation is a health issue rather than advisory ownership evidence. (iteration 4)\n@@\n - Path-count, top-level spread, and generic selective-commit warnings: sampled history shows they are common and do not prove contamination. (iteration 3)\n+- Generic push/account/worktree/multi-ref warnings and duplicate naming/permission advisories: positive local evidence or uncovered semantics are required. (iteration 4)\n@@\n - Predicting interactive/stdin-generated path selections, treating `--include` as suspicious, inferring ownership, or claiming the pre-hook candidate is the final commit tree. (iteration 3)\n+- Treating `gh` identity, API permission, leases, or remote snapshots as proof of Git transport identity, authorization, or final push outcome. (iteration 4)\n@@\n - Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows? (iteration 3)\n+- What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold? (iteration 4)\n@@\n ## 11. NEXT FOCUS\n-Investigate worktree, remote, and account coordination: detached/linked-worktree pushes, primary-checkout reconciliation, autosync bypass, active `gh` account versus remote owner, force/delete/tag refspecs, and exact preflight race/noise boundaries.\n+Calibrate the final candidate matrix and noise threshold: rank all retained rules by positive-evidence strength and overlap, measure available repository-history proxies, define one-line coalescing and latency budgets, and explicitly reject rules whose exact fire rate is unmeasured or likely too high.\n*** Update File: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n@@\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-prestate\",\n       \"text\": \"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n@@\n       \"id\": \"q-new-rules\",\n       \"text\": \"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n@@\n       \"id\": \"q-silence\",\n       \"text\": \"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable or too noisy?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n@@\n-  \"openQuestions\": [\"q-prestate\", \"q-new-rules\", \"q-noise\", \"q-silence\"],\n-  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\"q-operation-families\"],\n+  \"openQuestions\": [\"q-noise\"],\n+  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\"q-operation-families\", \"q-prestate\", \"q-new-rules\", \"q-silence\"],\n@@\n     \"Which staging and commit predicates detect cross-session contamination without warning on intentional multi-path commits?\",\n-    \"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\"\n+    \"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\",\n+    \"What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold?\"\n@@\n     {\n       \"id\": \"f3-no-ownership\",\n       \"iteration\": 3,\n       \"text\": \"Git state has no session-owner field and PreToolUse sees only the pre-hook candidate, so advisories must be neutral and cannot guarantee final commit contents.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f4-local-push-consequences\",\n+      \"iteration\": 4,\n+      \"text\": \"Detached or linked pushes to a branch checked out elsewhere and manual wrapper publication have predictable local coordination consequences, but push success and follower reconciliation remain unknown.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f4-uncovered-refspecs\",\n+      \"iteration\": 4,\n+      \"text\": \"Current pre-push enforcement skips tags and does not own force/delete/mirror/prune or destructive multi-ref semantics; these require positive destination evidence and coalesced advisories.\"\n@@\n     ,\"Predicting interactive or stdin path selections and hook-time final commit state\"\n+    ,\"Generic push, account, detached-worktree, and nondestructive multi-ref warnings\"\n+    ,\"Duplicating pre-push naming and permission blockers\"\n+    ,\"Treating gh identity, API permission, or network snapshots as remote outcome proof\"\n@@\n-    \"iterationsCompleted\": 3,\n-    \"openQuestions\": 4,\n-    \"resolvedQuestions\": 1,\n-    \"keyFindings\": 31,\n-    \"convergenceScore\": 0.72,\n-    \"coverageBySources\": {\"code\": 24, \"history\": 13, \"state\": 17, \"web\": 1}\n+    \"iterationsCompleted\": 4,\n+    \"openQuestions\": 1,\n+    \"resolvedQuestions\": 4,\n+    \"keyFindings\": 41,\n+    \"convergenceScore\": 0.75,\n+    \"coverageBySources\": {\"code\": 33, \"history\": 16, \"state\": 26, \"web\": 2, \"network\": 2}\n*** End Patch"},"output":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\nM .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json","metadata":{"diff":"Index: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n+++ /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n@@ -5,12 +5,12 @@\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n ## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\n - [x] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n-- [ ] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n-- [ ] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n+- [x] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n+- [x] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n - [ ] How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\n-- [ ] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n+- [x] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n <!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n \n ## 4. NON-GOALS\n - Do not implement or modify the evaluator, hook, sk-git rules, or frontmatter.\n@@ -26,39 +26,46 @@\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n ## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n - Which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution?\n+- Which local repository, worktree, index, config, ref, account, and remote snapshots are available, and which outcomes remain unknowable or raceable?\n+- Which high-cost destructive, staging, refspec, tag, and coordination failures beyond the briefing warrant candidate advisories?\n+- Which routine, recovery, enforced, ambiguous, or inherently post-execution cases should remain silent?\n <!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n \n <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\n <!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n ## 7. WHAT WORKED\n - Pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD reflog separated command risk from broad-family prevalence. (iteration 1)\n - Exact installed-Git help plus current-state probes separated destructive intent from routine forms already protected by Git and quantified current exposure without mutating state. (iteration 2)\n - Combining official selection semantics, native dry runs, current hooks, and 500-commit path breadth produced objective mismatch predicates without inventing session ownership. (iteration 3)\n+- Resolving actual worktree, destination, account, environment, hook, and ref state separated deterministic local push consequences from authorization guesses. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n ## 8. WHAT FAILED\n - Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it records ref outcomes rather than complete argv. (iteration 1)\n - Repeating reflog measurement could not recover flags, pathspecs, failures, or final state-gated fire rates; remote containment can prove known publication but not non-publication. (iteration 2)\n - Ordinary history cannot recover add/commit options or hook-time snapshots, and Git state has no session-owner field. (iteration 3)\n+- Push history has no argv/failure record, and network reads cannot close receive-time races; current missing hook installation is a health issue rather than advisory ownership evidence. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n ## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n - Generic warnings for every commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push: broad-family prevalence and existing enforcement make them noisy. (iteration 1)\n - Treating Git log/reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: flags, pathspecs, and failed commands are absent. (iteration 1)\n - Generic destructive-family warnings and recovery/control forms: exact state gates or Git's own protections make them unnecessary. (iteration 2)\n - Path-count, top-level spread, and generic selective-commit warnings: sampled history shows they are common and do not prove contamination. (iteration 3)\n+- Generic push/account/worktree/multi-ref warnings and duplicate naming/permission advisories: positive local evidence or uncovered semantics are required. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n ## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\n - Duplicate advisories for pre-push naming and non-allowlisted push permission already owned by blocking enforcement. (iteration 1)\n - Treating remote snapshots, push authorization, conflict outcomes, or post-hook commit contents as guaranteed pre-execution facts. (iteration 1)\n - Ordinary `branch -d`, checked-out branch deletion, ordinary worktree removal/prune, staged-only restore, clean/prune dry runs, routine GC, and rebase recovery forms. (iteration 2)\n - Predicting interactive/stdin-generated path selections, treating `--include` as suspicious, inferring ownership, or claiming the pre-hook candidate is the final commit tree. (iteration 3)\n+- Treating `gh` identity, API permission, leases, or remote snapshots as proof of Git transport identity, authorization, or final push outcome. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n ## 10A. SATURATED DIRECTIONS AND DIVERGENCE FRONTIER\n@@ -75,13 +82,14 @@\n - Which bounded state probes make published amend/rebase, destructive overwrite, branch/worktree deletion, and remote/tag force/delete predicates precise enough? (iteration 1)\n - How often do state-gated candidates match, given that ordinary Git history cannot provide exact argv telemetry? (iteration 1)\n - Which staging and commit predicates detect cross-session contamination without warning on intentional multi-path commits? (iteration 2)\n - Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows? (iteration 3)\n+- What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold? (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n ## 11. NEXT FOCUS\n-Investigate worktree, remote, and account coordination: detached/linked-worktree pushes, primary-checkout reconciliation, autosync bypass, active `gh` account versus remote owner, force/delete/tag refspecs, and exact preflight race/noise boundaries.\n+Calibrate the final candidate matrix and noise threshold: rank all retained rules by positive-evidence strength and overlap, measure available repository-history proxies, define one-line coalescing and latency budgets, and explicitly reject rules whose exact fire rate is unmeasured or likely too high.\n <!-- /ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: END -->\n \n ## 12. KNOWN CONTEXT\n\nIndex: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n+++ /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n@@ -13,14 +13,16 @@\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-prestate\",\n       \"text\": \"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-new-rules\",\n       \"text\": \"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-noise\",\n       \"text\": \"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\n@@ -28,18 +30,20 @@\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-silence\",\n       \"text\": \"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable or too noisy?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n     }\n   ],\n-  \"openQuestions\": [\"q-prestate\", \"q-new-rules\", \"q-noise\", \"q-silence\"],\n-  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\"q-operation-families\"],\n+  \"openQuestions\": [\"q-noise\"],\n+  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\"q-operation-families\", \"q-prestate\", \"q-new-rules\", \"q-silence\"],\n   \"carriedForwardOpenQuestions\": [\n     \"Which bounded state probes make destructive-history predicates precise enough?\",\n     \"How often do state-gated candidates match without exact argv telemetry?\",\n     \"Which staging and commit predicates detect cross-session contamination without warning on intentional multi-path commits?\",\n-    \"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\"\n+    \"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\",\n+    \"What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold?\"\n   ],\n   \"uncoveredQuestions\": [],\n   \"keyFindings\": [\n     {\n@@ -70,8 +74,18 @@\n     {\n       \"id\": \"f3-no-ownership\",\n       \"iteration\": 3,\n       \"text\": \"Git state has no session-owner field and PreToolUse sees only the pre-hook candidate, so advisories must be neutral and cannot guarantee final commit contents.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f4-local-push-consequences\",\n+      \"iteration\": 4,\n+      \"text\": \"Detached or linked pushes to a branch checked out elsewhere and manual wrapper publication have predictable local coordination consequences, but push success and follower reconciliation remain unknown.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f4-uncovered-refspecs\",\n+      \"iteration\": 4,\n+      \"text\": \"Current pre-push enforcement skips tags and does not own force/delete/mirror/prune or destructive multi-ref semantics; these require positive destination evidence and coalesced advisories.\"\n     }\n   ],\n   \"ruledOutDirections\": [\n     \"Unconditional warnings for generic commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push\",\n@@ -81,14 +95,17 @@\n     ,\"Generic destructive-family and recovery-form warnings\"\n     ,\"Using absent remote containment as proof that history is unpublished\"\n     ,\"Path-count-only, cross-top-level, and unconditional selective-commit warnings\"\n     ,\"Predicting interactive or stdin path selections and hook-time final commit state\"\n+    ,\"Generic push, account, detached-worktree, and nondestructive multi-ref warnings\"\n+    ,\"Duplicating pre-push naming and permission blockers\"\n+    ,\"Treating gh identity, API permission, or network snapshots as remote outcome proof\"\n   ],\n   \"metrics\": {\n-    \"iterationsCompleted\": 3,\n-    \"openQuestions\": 4,\n-    \"resolvedQuestions\": 1,\n-    \"keyFindings\": 31,\n-    \"convergenceScore\": 0.72,\n-    \"coverageBySources\": {\"code\": 24, \"history\": 13, \"state\": 17, \"web\": 1}\n+    \"iterationsCompleted\": 4,\n+    \"openQuestions\": 1,\n+    \"resolvedQuestions\": 4,\n+    \"keyFindings\": 41,\n+    \"convergenceScore\": 0.75,\n+    \"coverageBySources\": {\"code\": 33, \"history\": 16, \"state\": 26, \"web\": 2, \"network\": 2}\n   }\n }\n\n","files":[{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md","relativePath":".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md","type":"update","patch":"Index: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n+++ /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\n@@ -5,12 +5,12 @@\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n ## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\n - [x] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n-- [ ] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n-- [ ] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n+- [x] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n+- [x] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n - [ ] How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\n-- [ ] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n+- [x] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n <!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n \n ## 4. NON-GOALS\n - Do not implement or modify the evaluator, hook, sk-git rules, or frontmatter.\n@@ -26,39 +26,46 @@\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n ## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n - Which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution?\n+- Which local repository, worktree, index, config, ref, account, and remote snapshots are available, and which outcomes remain unknowable or raceable?\n+- Which high-cost destructive, staging, refspec, tag, and coordination failures beyond the briefing warrant candidate advisories?\n+- Which routine, recovery, enforced, ambiguous, or inherently post-execution cases should remain silent?\n <!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n \n <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\n <!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n ## 7. WHAT WORKED\n - Pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD reflog separated command risk from broad-family prevalence. (iteration 1)\n - Exact installed-Git help plus current-state probes separated destructive intent from routine forms already protected by Git and quantified current exposure without mutating state. (iteration 2)\n - Combining official selection semantics, native dry runs, current hooks, and 500-commit path breadth produced objective mismatch predicates without inventing session ownership. (iteration 3)\n+- Resolving actual worktree, destination, account, environment, hook, and ref state separated deterministic local push consequences from authorization guesses. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n ## 8. WHAT FAILED\n - Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it records ref outcomes rather than complete argv. (iteration 1)\n - Repeating reflog measurement could not recover flags, pathspecs, failures, or final state-gated fire rates; remote containment can prove known publication but not non-publication. (iteration 2)\n - Ordinary history cannot recover add/commit options or hook-time snapshots, and Git state has no session-owner field. (iteration 3)\n+- Push history has no argv/failure record, and network reads cannot close receive-time races; current missing hook installation is a health issue rather than advisory ownership evidence. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n ## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n - Generic warnings for every commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push: broad-family prevalence and existing enforcement make them noisy. (iteration 1)\n - Treating Git log/reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: flags, pathspecs, and failed commands are absent. (iteration 1)\n - Generic destructive-family warnings and recovery/control forms: exact state gates or Git's own protections make them unnecessary. (iteration 2)\n - Path-count, top-level spread, and generic selective-commit warnings: sampled history shows they are common and do not prove contamination. (iteration 3)\n+- Generic push/account/worktree/multi-ref warnings and duplicate naming/permission advisories: positive local evidence or uncovered semantics are required. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n ## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\n - Duplicate advisories for pre-push naming and non-allowlisted push permission already owned by blocking enforcement. (iteration 1)\n - Treating remote snapshots, push authorization, conflict outcomes, or post-hook commit contents as guaranteed pre-execution facts. (iteration 1)\n - Ordinary `branch -d`, checked-out branch deletion, ordinary worktree removal/prune, staged-only restore, clean/prune dry runs, routine GC, and rebase recovery forms. (iteration 2)\n - Predicting interactive/stdin-generated path selections, treating `--include` as suspicious, inferring ownership, or claiming the pre-hook candidate is the final commit tree. (iteration 3)\n+- Treating `gh` identity, API permission, leases, or remote snapshots as proof of Git transport identity, authorization, or final push outcome. (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n ## 10A. SATURATED DIRECTIONS AND DIVERGENCE FRONTIER\n@@ -75,13 +82,14 @@\n - Which bounded state probes make published amend/rebase, destructive overwrite, branch/worktree deletion, and remote/tag force/delete predicates precise enough? (iteration 1)\n - How often do state-gated candidates match, given that ordinary Git history cannot provide exact argv telemetry? (iteration 1)\n - Which staging and commit predicates detect cross-session contamination without warning on intentional multi-path commits? (iteration 2)\n - Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows? (iteration 3)\n+- What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold? (iteration 4)\n <!-- /ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n \n <!-- ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n ## 11. NEXT FOCUS\n-Investigate worktree, remote, and account coordination: detached/linked-worktree pushes, primary-checkout reconciliation, autosync bypass, active `gh` account versus remote owner, force/delete/tag refspecs, and exact preflight race/noise boundaries.\n+Calibrate the final candidate matrix and noise threshold: rank all retained rules by positive-evidence strength and overlap, measure available repository-history proxies, define one-line coalescing and latency budgets, and explicitly reject rules whose exact fire rate is unmeasured or likely too high.\n <!-- /ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: END -->\n \n ## 12. KNOWN CONTEXT\n","additions":12,"deletions":4},{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json","relativePath":".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json","type":"update","patch":"Index: /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n===================================================================\n--- /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n+++ /Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json\n@@ -13,14 +13,16 @@\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-prestate\",\n       \"text\": \"Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-new-rules\",\n       \"text\": \"Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-noise\",\n       \"text\": \"How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\",\n@@ -28,18 +30,20 @@\n     },\n     {\n       \"id\": \"q-silence\",\n       \"text\": \"Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable or too noisy?\",\n-      \"resolved\": false\n+      \"resolved\": true,\n+      \"resolvedAtIteration\": 4\n     }\n   ],\n-  \"openQuestions\": [\"q-prestate\", \"q-new-rules\", \"q-noise\", \"q-silence\"],\n-  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\"q-operation-families\"],\n+  \"openQuestions\": [\"q-noise\"],\n+  \"resolvedQuestions\": [\"q-operation-families\", \"q-prestate\", \"q-new-rules\", \"q-silence\"],\n   \"carriedForwardOpenQuestions\": [\n     \"Which bounded state probes make destructive-history predicates precise enough?\",\n     \"How often do state-gated candidates match without exact argv telemetry?\",\n     \"Which staging and commit predicates detect cross-session contamination without warning on intentional multi-path commits?\",\n-    \"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\"\n+    \"Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows?\",\n+    \"What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold?\"\n   ],\n   \"uncoveredQuestions\": [],\n   \"keyFindings\": [\n     {\n@@ -70,8 +74,18 @@\n     {\n       \"id\": \"f3-no-ownership\",\n       \"iteration\": 3,\n       \"text\": \"Git state has no session-owner field and PreToolUse sees only the pre-hook candidate, so advisories must be neutral and cannot guarantee final commit contents.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f4-local-push-consequences\",\n+      \"iteration\": 4,\n+      \"text\": \"Detached or linked pushes to a branch checked out elsewhere and manual wrapper publication have predictable local coordination consequences, but push success and follower reconciliation remain unknown.\"\n+    },\n+    {\n+      \"id\": \"f4-uncovered-refspecs\",\n+      \"iteration\": 4,\n+      \"text\": \"Current pre-push enforcement skips tags and does not own force/delete/mirror/prune or destructive multi-ref semantics; these require positive destination evidence and coalesced advisories.\"\n     }\n   ],\n   \"ruledOutDirections\": [\n     \"Unconditional warnings for generic commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push\",\n@@ -81,14 +95,17 @@\n     ,\"Generic destructive-family and recovery-form warnings\"\n     ,\"Using absent remote containment as proof that history is unpublished\"\n     ,\"Path-count-only, cross-top-level, and unconditional selective-commit warnings\"\n     ,\"Predicting interactive or stdin path selections and hook-time final commit state\"\n+    ,\"Generic push, account, detached-worktree, and nondestructive multi-ref warnings\"\n+    ,\"Duplicating pre-push naming and permission blockers\"\n+    ,\"Treating gh identity, API permission, or network snapshots as remote outcome proof\"\n   ],\n   \"metrics\": {\n-    \"iterationsCompleted\": 3,\n-    \"openQuestions\": 4,\n-    \"resolvedQuestions\": 1,\n-    \"keyFindings\": 31,\n-    \"convergenceScore\": 0.72,\n-    \"coverageBySources\": {\"code\": 24, \"history\": 13, \"state\": 17, \"web\": 1}\n+    \"iterationsCompleted\": 4,\n+    \"openQuestions\": 1,\n+    \"resolvedQuestions\": 4,\n+    \"keyFindings\": 41,\n+    \"convergenceScore\": 0.75,\n+    \"coverageBySources\": {\"code\": 33, \"history\": 16, \"state\": 26, \"web\": 2, \"network\": 2}\n   }\n }\n","additions":29,"deletions":12}],"diagnostics":{},"truncated":false},"title":"Success. Updated the following files:\nM .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md\nM .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/findings-registry.json","time":{"start":1785180986218,"end":1785180986230}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_045596d8cd3e2fd8016a67b31b93248191b91ed6ba3779f3ea"}},"id":"prt_fa513a3cd001XicRGzBHbd57Y9","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa513840f001EPco5PX0oJ5GN5"}}
