{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785181607817,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"call_OvW8g5GHtXHXRJNK12AvO9n2","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md","offset":1,"limit":150},"output":"<path>/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: # Deep Research Strategy: Git Preflight Advisory Rules\n2: \n3: ## 2. TOPIC\n4: Determine which git invocation shapes justify a one-line pre-execution advisory, which repository and command state is genuinely available before execution, and which candidate rules remain quiet enough to preserve operator attention.\n5: \n6: <!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n7: ## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\n8: - [x] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n9: - [x] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n10: - [x] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n11: - [ ] How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\n12: - [x] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n13: <!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n14: \n15: ## 4. NON-GOALS\n16: - Do not implement or modify the evaluator, hook, sk-git rules, or frontmatter.\n17: - Do not redesign the advisory mechanism.\n18: - Do not turn advisory findings into blocking rules.\n19: - Do not claim post-execution outcomes are pre-evaluable.\n20: - Do not write outside this detached lineage directory.\n21: \n22: ## 5. STOP CONDITIONS\n23: - Run exactly five iterations; convergence before iteration five is telemetry only.\n24: - Stop after iteration five when candidate operations include exact invocation shapes, genuinely available pre-state, measured or explicitly unmeasured noise, source classification, and confirmed/inferred confidence.\n25: - Preserve eliminated high-noise and non-pre-evaluable alternatives as primary negative knowledge.\n26: \n27: <!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n28: ## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n29: - Which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution?\n30: - Which local repository, worktree, index, config, ref, account, and remote snapshots are available, and which outcomes remain unknowable or raceable?\n31: - Which high-cost destructive, staging, refspec, tag, and coordination failures beyond the briefing warrant candidate advisories?\n32: - Which routine, recovery, enforced, ambiguous, or inherently post-execution cases should remain silent?\n33: <!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n34: \n35: <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\n36: <!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n37: ## 7. WHAT WORKED\n38: - Pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD reflog separated command risk from broad-family prevalence. (iteration 1)\n39: - Exact installed-Git help plus current-state probes separated destructive intent from routine forms already protected by Git and quantified current exposure without mutating state. (iteration 2)\n40: - Combining official selection semantics, native dry runs, current hooks, and 500-commit path breadth produced objective mismatch predicates without inventing session ownership. (iteration 3)\n41: - Resolving actual worktree, destination, account, environment, hook, and ref state separated deterministic local push consequences from authorization guesses. (iteration 4)\n42: <!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n43: \n44: <!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n45: ## 8. WHAT FAILED\n46: - Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it records ref outcomes rather than complete argv. (iteration 1)\n47: - Repeating reflog measurement could not recover flags, pathspecs, failures, or final state-gated fire rates; remote containment can prove known publication but not non-publication. (iteration 2)\n48: - Ordinary history cannot recover add/commit options or hook-time snapshots, and Git state has no session-owner field. (iteration 3)\n49: - Push history has no argv/failure record, and network reads cannot close receive-time races; current missing hook installation is a health issue rather than advisory ownership evidence. (iteration 4)\n50: <!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n51: \n52: <!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n53: ## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n54: - Generic warnings for every commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push: broad-family prevalence and existing enforcement make them noisy. (iteration 1)\n55: - Treating Git log/reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: flags, pathspecs, and failed commands are absent. (iteration 1)\n56: - Generic destructive-family warnings and recovery/control forms: exact state gates or Git's own protections make them unnecessary. (iteration 2)\n57: - Path-count, top-level spread, and generic selective-commit warnings: sampled history shows they are common and do not prove contamination. (iteration 3)\n58: - Generic push/account/worktree/multi-ref warnings and duplicate naming/permission advisories: positive local evidence or uncovered semantics are required. (iteration 4)\n59: <!-- /ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n60: \n61: <!-- ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n62: ## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\n63: - Duplicate advisories for pre-push naming and non-allowlisted push permission already owned by blocking enforcement. (iteration 1)\n64: - Treating remote snapshots, push authorization, conflict outcomes, or post-hook commit contents as guaranteed pre-execution facts. (iteration 1)\n65: - Ordinary `branch -d`, checked-out branch deletion, ordinary worktree removal/prune, staged-only restore, clean/prune dry runs, routine GC, and rebase recovery forms. (iteration 2)\n66: - Predicting interactive/stdin-generated path selections, treating `--include` as suspicious, inferring ownership, or claiming the pre-hook candidate is the final commit tree. (iteration 3)\n67: - Treating `gh` identity, API permission, leases, or remote snapshots as proof of Git transport identity, authorization, or final push outcome. (iteration 4)\n68: <!-- /ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n69: \n70: <!-- ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n71: ## 10A. SATURATED DIRECTIONS AND DIVERGENCE FRONTIER\n72: - Completed pivots: 0\n73: - Failed pivots: 0\n74: - Audited overrides: 0\n75: - Saturated: none yet\n76: - Pivot lineage: none yet\n77: - Remaining frontier: operation inventory, destructive history, staging/commit semantics, coordination/remotes, noise calibration\n78: <!-- /ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n79: \n80: <!-- ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n81: ## 11A. CARRIED-FORWARD OPEN QUESTIONS\n82: - Which bounded state probes make published amend/rebase, destructive overwrite, branch/worktree deletion, and remote/tag force/delete predicates precise enough? (iteration 1)\n83: - How often do state-gated candidates match, given that ordinary Git history cannot provide exact argv telemetry? (iteration 1)\n84: - Which staging and commit predicates detect cross-session contamination without warning on intentional multi-path commits? (iteration 2)\n85: - Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows? (iteration 3)\n86: - What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold? (iteration 4)\n87: <!-- /ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n88: \n89: <!-- ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n90: ## 11. NEXT FOCUS\n91: Calibrate the final candidate matrix and noise threshold: rank all retained rules by positive-evidence strength and overlap, measure available repository-history proxies, define one-line coalescing and latency budgets, and explicitly reject rules whose exact fire rate is unmeasured or likely too high.\n92: <!-- /ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n93: <!-- MACHINE-OWNED: END -->\n94: \n95: ## 12. KNOWN CONTEXT\n96: - The briefing's five incidents are a floor, not the answer.\n97: - Existing evaluator checks are pure command-string predicates and `sk-git` currently declares no `hard_rules`.\n98: - Every usable finding must include operation, pre-execution state and availability, measured or explicitly unmeasured noise, source, and confidence.\n99: - `resource-map.md` is absent; coverage comes from local source, Git history, and exact repository-state measurements.\n100: - The code graph is empty, so exact Grep, Read, Glob, and bounded Git evidence are authoritative.\n101: \n102: ## 13. RESEARCH BOUNDARIES\n103: - Max iterations: 5\n104: - Stop policy: max-iterations\n105: - Convergence threshold: 0.05 telemetry only before iteration 5\n106: - Executor: `cli-opencode`, model `openai/gpt-5.6-sol`\n107: - Allowed writes: this detached lineage directory only\n108: - Progressive synthesis: false; phase synthesis owns `research.md`\n\n(End of file - total 108 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"# Deep Research Strategy: Git Preflight Advisory Rules\n\n## 2. TOPIC\nDetermine which git invocation shapes justify a one-line pre-execution advisory, which repository and command state is genuinely available before execution, and which candidate rules remain quiet enough to preserve operator attention.\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\n- [x] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n- [x] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n- [x] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n- [ ] How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\n- [x] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n<!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n\n## 4. NON-GOALS\n- Do not implement or modify the evaluator, hook, sk-git rules, or frontmatter.\n- Do not redesign the advisory mechanism.\n- Do not turn advisory findings into blocking rules.\n- Do not claim post-execution outcomes are pre-evaluable.\n- Do not write outside this detached lineage directory.","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md","text":"# Deep Research Strategy: Git Preflight Advisory Rules\n\n## 2. TOPIC\nDetermine which git invocation shapes justify a one-line pre-execution advisory, which repository and command state is genuinely available before execution, and which candidate rules remain quiet enough to preserve operator attention.\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n## 3. KEY QUESTIONS (remaining)\n- [x] Which git operation families can be classified reliably from the Bash command before execution?\n- [x] Which repository, worktree, index, remote, config, and account state is genuinely available before each candidate command runs?\n- [x] Which high-cost failures beyond the five observed incidents warrant new advisory rules?\n- [ ] How often would each candidate fire in real repository history, and what is the defensible noise threshold?\n- [x] Which dangerous-looking operations should remain silent because enforcement already owns them or preflight state is unavailable/too noisy?\n<!-- /ANCHOR:key-questions -->\n\n## 4. NON-GOALS\n- Do not implement or modify the evaluator, hook, sk-git rules, or frontmatter.\n- Do not redesign the advisory mechanism.\n- Do not turn advisory findings into blocking rules.\n- Do not claim post-execution outcomes are pre-evaluable.\n- Do not write outside this detached lineage directory.\n\n## 5. STOP CONDITIONS\n- Run exactly five iterations; convergence before iteration five is telemetry only.\n- Stop after iteration five when candidate operations include exact invocation shapes, genuinely available pre-state, measured or explicitly unmeasured noise, source classification, and confirmed/inferred confidence.\n- Preserve eliminated high-noise and non-pre-evaluable alternatives as primary negative knowledge.\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n## 6. ANSWERED QUESTIONS\n- Which direct Git operation families can be classified reliably from Bash command text before execution?\n- Which local repository, worktree, index, config, ref, account, and remote snapshots are available, and which outcomes remain unknowable or raceable?\n- Which high-cost destructive, staging, refspec, tag, and coordination failures beyond the briefing warrant candidate advisories?\n- Which routine, recovery, enforced, ambiguous, or inherently post-execution cases should remain silent?\n<!-- /ANCHOR:answered-questions -->\n\n<!-- MACHINE-OWNED: START -->\n<!-- ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n## 7. WHAT WORKED\n- Pairing exact sk-git rules and hook coverage with a bounded 1,482-entry HEAD reflog separated command risk from broad-family prevalence. (iteration 1)\n- Exact installed-Git help plus current-state probes separated destructive intent from routine forms already protected by Git and quantified current exposure without mutating state. (iteration 2)\n- Combining official selection semantics, native dry runs, current hooks, and 500-commit path breadth produced objective mismatch predicates without inventing session ownership. (iteration 3)\n- Resolving actual worktree, destination, account, environment, hook, and ref state separated deterministic local push consequences from authorization guesses. (iteration 4)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:what-worked -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n## 8. WHAT FAILED\n- Git history cannot recover option-sensitive invocation frequency or failed commands because it records ref outcomes rather than complete argv. (iteration 1)\n- Repeating reflog measurement could not recover flags, pathspecs, failures, or final state-gated fire rates; remote containment can prove known publication but not non-publication. (iteration 2)\n- Ordinary history cannot recover add/commit options or hook-time snapshots, and Git state has no session-owner field. (iteration 3)\n- Push history has no argv/failure record, and network reads cannot close receive-time races; current missing hook installation is a health issue rather than advisory ownership evidence. (iteration 4)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:what-failed -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n## 9. EXHAUSTED APPROACHES (do not retry)\n- Generic warnings for every commit, reset, rebase, pull, or push: broad-family prevalence and existing enforcement make them noisy. (iteration 1)\n- Treating Git log/reflog as exact shell-command telemetry: flags, pathspecs, and failed commands are absent. (iteration 1)\n- Generic destructive-family warnings and recovery/control forms: exact state gates or Git's own protections make them unnecessary. (iteration 2)\n- Path-count, top-level spread, and generic selective-commit warnings: sampled history shows they are common and do not prove contamination. (iteration 3)\n- Generic push/account/worktree/multi-ref warnings and duplicate naming/permission advisories: positive local evidence or uncovered semantics are required. (iteration 4)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:exhausted-approaches -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n## 10. RULED OUT DIRECTIONS\n- Duplicate advisories for pre-push naming and non-allowlisted push permission already owned by blocking enforcement. (iteration 1)\n- Treating remote snapshots, push authorization, conflict outcomes, or post-hook commit contents as guaranteed pre-execution facts. (iteration 1)\n- Ordinary `branch -d`, checked-out branch deletion, ordinary worktree removal/prune, staged-only restore, clean/prune dry runs, routine GC, and rebase recovery forms. (iteration 2)\n- Predicting interactive/stdin-generated path selections, treating `--include` as suspicious, inferring ownership, or claiming the pre-hook candidate is the final commit tree. (iteration 3)\n- Treating `gh` identity, API permission, leases, or remote snapshots as proof of Git transport identity, authorization, or final push outcome. (iteration 4)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:ruled-out-directions -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n## 10A. SATURATED DIRECTIONS AND DIVERGENCE FRONTIER\n- Completed pivots: 0\n- Failed pivots: 0\n- Audited overrides: 0\n- Saturated: none yet\n- Pivot lineage: none yet\n- Remaining frontier: operation inventory, destructive history, staging/commit semantics, coordination/remotes, noise calibration\n<!-- /ANCHOR:divergence-frontier -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n## 11A. CARRIED-FORWARD OPEN QUESTIONS\n- Which bounded state probes make published amend/rebase, destructive overwrite, branch/worktree deletion, and remote/tag force/delete predicates precise enough? (iteration 1)\n- How often do state-gated candidates match, given that ordinary Git history cannot provide exact argv telemetry? (iteration 1)\n- Which staging and commit predicates detect cross-session contamination without warning on intentional multi-path commits? (iteration 2)\n- Which positive local coordination and remote/account facts justify advisories despite unavoidable race windows? (iteration 3)\n- What final candidate tiers, coalescing rules, latency limits, and aggregate alert budget keep advisories below the attention threshold? (iteration 4)\n<!-- /ANCHOR:carried-forward-open-questions -->\n\n<!-- ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n## 11. NEXT FOCUS\nCalibrate the final candidate matrix and noise threshold: rank all retained rules by positive-evidence strength and overlap, measure available repository-history proxies, define one-line coalescing and latency budgets, and explicitly reject rules whose exact fire rate is unmeasured or likely too high.\n<!-- /ANCHOR:next-focus -->\n<!-- MACHINE-OWNED: END -->\n\n## 12. KNOWN CONTEXT\n- The briefing's five incidents are a floor, not the answer.\n- Existing evaluator checks are pure command-string predicates and `sk-git` currently declares no `hard_rules`.\n- Every usable finding must include operation, pre-execution state and availability, measured or explicitly unmeasured noise, source, and confidence.\n- `resource-map.md` is absent; coverage comes from local source, Git history, and exact repository-state measurements.\n- The code graph is empty, so exact Grep, Read, Glob, and bounded Git evidence are authoritative.\n\n## 13. RESEARCH BOUNDARIES\n- Max iterations: 5\n- Stop policy: max-iterations\n- Convergence threshold: 0.05 telemetry only before iteration 5\n- Executor: `cli-opencode`, model `openai/gpt-5.6-sol`\n- Allowed writes: this detached lineage directory only\n- Progressive synthesis: false; phase synthesis owns `research.md`","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":108,"totalLines":108,"truncated":false}},"title":".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/deep-research-strategy.md","time":{"start":1785181607796,"end":1785181607814}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_099f8bf1cb61ad5f016a67b5a6cac081919e68d80570b1c803"}},"id":"prt_fa51d93eb001gebFSGMQARaeU7","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa51d6d54001UoxddTNc4reP1W"}}
