{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785181839203,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_6UMUAKWrlZ36Lu7E4kNzgvz4","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"node -e 'const fs=require(\"fs\"),p=\".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol\"; for(const f of [\"deep-research-config.json\",\"findings-registry.json\"]){JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(`${p}/${f}`,\"utf8\"));} const state=fs.readFileSync(`${p}/deep-research-state.jsonl`,\"utf8\").trim().split(/\\n/).map(JSON.parse); const it=state.filter(x=>x.type===\"iteration\"); if(it.length!==5) throw new Error(`iterations=${it.length}`); if(!state.some(x=>x.event===\"synthesis_complete\"&&x.stopReason===\"maxIterationsReached\")) throw new Error(\"missing synthesis event\"); for(let i=1;i<=5;i++){const n=String(i).padStart(3,\"0\"), md=`${p}/iterations/iteration-${n}.md`, d=`${p}/deltas/iter-${n}.jsonl`; if(!fs.existsSync(md)||fs.statSync(md).size===0) throw new Error(`missing ${md}`); const rows=fs.readFileSync(d,\"utf8\").trim().split(/\\n/).map(JSON.parse); if(!rows.some(x=>x.type===\"iteration\"&&x.iteration===i)) throw new Error(`bad ${d}`);} for(const f of [\"deep-research-strategy.md\",\"deep-research-dashboard.md\",\"research.md\",\"resource-map.md\"]){if(!fs.existsSync(`${p}/${f}`)||fs.statSync(`${p}/${f}`).size===0) throw new Error(`missing ${f}`);} const cfg=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(`${p}/deep-research-config.json`,\"utf8\")); if(cfg.status!==\"complete\") throw new Error(\"config not complete\"); console.log(JSON.stringify({ok:true,iterations:it.length,stateRecords:state.length,status:cfg.status}));'","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public"},"output":"{\"ok\":true,\"iterations\":5,\"stateRecords\":8,\"status\":\"complete\"}\n","metadata":{"output":"{\"ok\":true,\"iterations\":5,\"stateRecords\":8,\"status\":\"complete\"}\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"node -e 'const fs=require(\"fs\"),p=\".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol\"; for(const f of [\"deep-research-config.json\",\"findings-registry.json\"]){JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(`${p}/${f}`,\"utf8\"));} const state=fs.readFileSync(`${p}/deep-research-state.jsonl`,\"utf8\").trim().split(/\\n/).map(JSON.parse); const it=state.filter(x=>x.type===\"iteration\"); if(it.length!==5) throw new Error(`iterations=${it.length}`); if(!state.some(x=>x.event===\"synthesis_complete\"&&x.stopReason===\"maxIterationsReached\")) throw new Error(\"missing synthesis event\"); for(let i=1;i<=5;i++){const n=String(i).padStart(3,\"0\"), md=`${p}/iterations/iteration-${n}.md`, d=`${p}/deltas/iter-${n}.jsonl`; if(!fs.existsSync(md)||fs.statSync(md).size===0) throw new Error(`missing ${md}`); const rows=fs.readFileSync(d,\"utf8\").trim().split(/\\n/).map(JSON.parse); if(!rows.some(x=>x.type===\"iteration\"&&x.iteration===i)) throw new Error(`bad ${d}`);} for(const f of [\"deep-research-strategy.md\",\"deep-research-dashboard.md\",\"research.md\",\"resource-map.md\"]){if(!fs.existsSync(`${p}/${f}`)||fs.statSync(`${p}/${f}`).size===0) throw new Error(`missing ${f}`);} const cfg=JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync(`${p}/deep-research-config.json`,\"utf8\")); if(cfg.status!==\"complete\") throw new Error(\"config not complete\"); console.log(JSON.stringify({ok:true,iterations:it.length,stateRecords:state.length,status:cfg.status}));'","time":{"start":1785181839195,"end":1785181839200}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_02fb8a138c9380a4016a67b68eec5c8191becb40624cff6cb7"}},"id":"prt_fa5211ef5001Qfu3A7HMu1AvKG","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa520dc52001cIctQuYqyqgDE7"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785181839219,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"call_S2bFQXeaZGsI7Gj7WmrcPu6s","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"git status --short -- \".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol\"","workdir":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public"},"output":"?? .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/\n","metadata":{"output":"?? .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"git status --short -- \".opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol\"","time":{"start":1785181839214,"end":1785181839217}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_02fb8a138c9380a4016a67b68eec9081919eb9b9cf24dc02f3"}},"id":"prt_fa5211f43001BC1KjAbYOx21Bk","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa520dc52001cIctQuYqyqgDE7"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1785181839292,"sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"grep","callID":"call_Fy4kcmVwYDvLuAosZV2YoiYM","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"pattern":"\\*\\*Operation:\\*\\*|\\| Operation \\| Pre-state|Pre-state and genuine availability|Noise / source / confidence","path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol","include":"*.md"},"output":"Found 44 matches\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/research.md:\n  Line 38: | Operation | Pre-state and availability | Noise | Source | Confidence |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-002.md:\n  Line 9: 1. **Published amend remains a high-value, positive-evidence advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit ... --amend ...`, including `git commit --amend --no-edit`; ordinary commits and `--fixup=amend:*` are not the same operation. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** resolve `HEAD`, then run `git for-each-ref --contains=HEAD` over `refs/remotes` and other local integration refs, excluding only the current branch itself. The current `HEAD` (`b1024adc...`) is contained by `refs/remotes/origin/skilled/v4.0.0.0`, so the predicate would match now. **Genuinely available:** positive local containment is available before execution; a missing match is only unknown because remote-tracking refs may be stale and another clone's consumption is unknowable. **Noise:** 7 amend outcomes among 1,486 HEAD-reflog entries (0.47%); the published subset is not measurable from reflog argv and must remain explicitly unmeasured. **Source classification:** named sk-git NEVER #10. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for command shape, named rule, local positive state, and broad rate; inferred for completeness of “published.” Validate the candidate only on positive containment, never as an unconditional commit warning. **Race window:** a remote or local ref can move after the probe. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:491-493] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git for-each-ref --format=%(refname) --contains=HEAD refs/remotes refs/heads`; `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`; `git commit -h`]\n\n  Line 11: 2. **Published rebase is probeable for explicit rewrite sets, but `pull --rebase` cannot be predicted exactly before its internal fetch.** **Operation:** `git rebase [--onto <newbase>] <upstream> [<branch>]` after excluding recovery/control forms such as `--abort`, `--continue`, `--skip`, `--quit`, and `--show-current-patch`; also `git pull --rebase` or config-induced pull-rebase. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** for an explicit rebase, derive the commits to replay from the parsed upstream/fork point and intersect them with commits reachable from `refs/remotes`; separately read dirty tracked state and `rebase.autoStash`/`pull.rebase`. **Genuinely available:** the explicit local rewrite set is available; for `pull --rebase`, the command's fetch can change the upstream after PreToolUse, so only already-known publication and dirty/autostash risk are available. **Noise:** 44 rebase outcomes among 1,486 reflog entries (2.96%); the published subset and option/config subsets are not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git NEVER #8 and ALWAYS #14. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for the rule, local inputs, installed `--autostash` behavior, and broad rate; inferred for the final intersection predicate. Validate state-gated explicit rebase, reject generic rebase warnings—especially warnings on recovery forms. **Race window:** refs and the worktree can change; `pull --rebase` adds an unavoidable fetch-to-rebase window. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:326-328] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:491-493] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git rebase -h`; `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n  Line 13: 3. **`reset --hard` needs two independent risk gates: uncommitted overwrite and branch-tip retreat.** **Operation:** `git reset --hard [<tree-ish>]`; do not match `git reset` in other modes. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** resolve the target (default `HEAD`), use read-only diffs to test whether index/worktree content differs from it, inspect unmerged entries, and count commits in `<target>..HEAD` that lack another durable local/remote ref. The current tree has 44 tracked paths differing from `HEAD`, 0 staged paths, and 137 total status paths, so `git reset --hard HEAD` would match the content-loss gate now. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution, including the target's current object and current diffs; untracked paths are affected only in collision cases and must not be broadly claimed from status alone. **Noise:** 201 reset outcomes among 1,486 reflog entries (13.53%); mode-specific hard-reset frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** new, justified by immediate uncommitted-content loss and by sk-git ALWAYS #15's prohibition on resetting a dirty/concurrent primary tree. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for installed `--hard` semantics and current counts; inferred for the durable-ref suppression. Validate only when either gate is nonempty; reject every-reset warnings. **Race window:** concurrent edits or ref movement after the diff can invalidate the snapshot. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-327] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git reset -h`; `git status --porcelain=v1 --untracked-files=all`; `git diff --name-only HEAD --`; `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n  Line 15: 4. **`git clean` has an exact built-in preflight, so warnings should depend on its dry-run result.** **Operation:** destructive forms `git clean -f|-ff` with the same `-d`, `-x`/`-X`, exclusions, and pathspec as the proposed command; `git clean -n`/`--dry-run` is explicitly excluded. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** replace destructive force with `-n` while preserving selection flags/pathspec and warn only if Git reports at least one removal. On the current repository, `git clean -nd` reports 84 candidates and `git clean -ndx` reports 611, showing why `-x` materially changes risk. **Genuinely available:** yes; Git itself applies ignore and pathspec semantics in the dry run. **Noise:** invocation frequency is not measured because command flags are absent from history; the present-state match is measured, not a historical fire rate. **Source classification:** new, justified by deletion of untracked/ignored content. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed. Validate `clean -f*` plus nonempty dry-run; eliminate no-force and dry-run forms because Git's force requirement or read-only mode already protects them. **Race window:** untracked files can appear or disappear between dry run and execution. [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git clean -h`; `git clean -nd`; `git clean -ndx`]\n\n  Line 17: 5. **Worktree restore is advisory-worthy only when it intersects an actual tracked delta; staged-only restore should remain silent.** **Operation:** `git restore [--worktree] [--source=<tree-ish>] -- <pathspec>...` and legacy `git checkout [<tree-ish>] -- <pathspec>...`; exclude branch checkout and `git restore --staged` without `--worktree`. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** for default worktree restore, test unstaged deltas against the index for the exact pathspec; for `--source=<tree-ish>`, compare the selected paths against that source; if both `--staged --worktree` are present, include index differences. The current tree has 44 tracked paths differing from `HEAD`, so only matching pathspecs should trigger. **Genuinely available:** yes locally, but Git has no native restore dry-run, so the evaluator must faithfully preserve source and pathspec semantics. **Noise:** not measured; reflogs do not record path restores. **Source classification:** new, justified by silent overwrite of tracked worktree content. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for installed restore modes and current delta; inferred for a parser-equivalent impact probe. Validate exact worktree-overwrite matches; eliminate staged-only unstage operations as recoverable routine use. **Race window:** edits after the diff can create a false negative. [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git restore -h`; `git diff --name-only HEAD --`]\n\n  Line 19: 6. **Force branch deletion should warn only for non-checked-out branches with unique commits; Git already owns two safer cases.** **Operation:** `git branch -D <branch>...` or `git branch --delete --force <branch>...`; ordinary `-d` is excluded. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** map checked-out branches with `git worktree list --porcelain`, resolve each target, and count commits not in the live primary `HEAD`, its upstream, or another durable ref. Current measurements: 46 local branches, 34 checked out, and 13 non-merged branches with commits absent from the primary `HEAD`. **Genuinely available:** yes locally. **Noise:** deletion invocation frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #17 plus a new unique-commit refinement; the discard workflow documents `-D` only after explicit confirmation. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for `-d` versus `-D`, current branch/worktree state, and required cleanup ordering; inferred for which comparison refs represent all valuable integration tips. Validate `-D` only when an unoccupied target has unique commits. Eliminate ordinary `-d` because Git checks mergedness, and eliminate the checked-out-branch subcase because Git refuses deletion while a worktree owns it. **Race window:** a worktree may attach after the probe, but Git's execution-time refusal remains the final guard. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/shared-patterns.md:197-201] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:258-281] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git branch -h`; `git worktree list --porcelain`; `git merge-base --is-ancestor`; `git rev-list --count HEAD..<branch>`]\n\n  Line 21: 7. **Forced worktree removal is valuable only for dirty or positively active targets; routine cleanup and `worktree prune` should not warn.** **Operation:** `git worktree remove -f|--force <path>`; distinguish this from ordinary `git worktree remove <path>` and `git worktree prune`. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** resolve the registered target, check tracked/untracked status, branch/detachment, merge relation to the primary checkout's live `HEAD`, lock state, and a wrapper-session marker with a live PID. Of 35 registered worktrees, 20 are dirty and 15 clean; one is detached. **Genuinely available:** cleanliness and registration are available; positive wrapper liveness is available when the marker is readable, while missing markers cannot prove inactivity. **Noise:** invocation frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #17. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed. Validate forced removal when the target is dirty or positively live. Eliminate ordinary dirty removal because Git requires force, ordinary clean removal because sk-git uses it as normal cleanup, and `git worktree prune` because it clears stale administration rather than deleting a present worktree. The existing reaper already restricts automatic removal to clean, merged, proven-inactive wrapper worktrees and reports all other lanes. **Race window:** status, PID liveness, and registration can change after the probe; a PID can also be reused. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh:64-88] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/worktree-reaper.sh:136-186] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git worktree -h`; `git worktree list --porcelain`; per-worktree `git status --porcelain=v1`]\n\n  Line 23: 8. **Stash destruction is not generally protected by the autostash guard; gate it on the exact target's durable rescue.** **Operation:** `git stash drop [<stash>]` and `git stash clear`; exclude `stash apply`, and exclude `stash pop` because Git keeps the entry when application conflicts. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** resolve the symbolic selector to a SHA immediately before execution, enumerate `refs/autostash-rescue/*` and other durable refs for that SHA, and for `clear` count unrescued entries. The repository has 20 stash entries, one autostash-rescue ref, and 0 current stash entries directly anchored by that rescue ref—20 current entries are unrescued by this mechanism. **Genuinely available:** yes before deletion, but `stash@{n}` is a moving selector under concurrent stash operations. **Noise:** command frequency is not measured; current exposure is measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #14, extended to distinguish ordinary stash entries from guarded autostashes. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for guard scope and current counts; inferred for suppression when another durable ref reaches the stash commit. Validate drop/clear when selected entries lack durable rescue; suppress or lower the advisory for a matching rescue ref to avoid duplicating the existing hook. **Race window:** another process can push/drop a stash and renumber selectors after the probe. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:326-326] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/lib/autostash-orphan-guard.sh:14-43] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git stash -h`; `git stash list --format=%H`; `git for-each-ref refs/autostash-rescue`]\n\n  Line 25: 9. **Immediate reflog expiry has an exact dry-run and should warn about pointer loss, not claim immediate object deletion.** **Operation:** `git reflog expire --expire=now ...`, `--expire-unreachable=now`, especially with `--all`; ordinary expiry and `--dry-run` are excluded. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** rerun the selected refs with `--dry-run --verbose` to count entries that Git would prune, and only after the exact token match optionally compare `git rev-list --reflog --all` with `git rev-list --all`. The current repository has 390 commits reachable through reflogs but not durable refs. **Genuinely available:** yes as a local snapshot. **Noise:** invocation frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** new, justified because immediate expiry destroys recovery pointers even though objects may survive until later pruning. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for installed dry-run support and the 390-commit exposure; inferred for which reflog-only commits are operator-important. Validate immediate-expiry forms with nonempty dry-run; eliminate default expiry and dry-run invocations. **Race window:** refs and reflogs can change between probe and execution. [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git reflog expire -h`; set difference of `git rev-list --reflog --all` and `git rev-list --all`]\n\n  Line 27: 10. **Immediate object pruning warrants a command-shape advisory; an expensive reachability scan cannot certify safety under concurrent writers.** **Operation:** `git gc --prune=now` and `git prune --expire=now`; exclude `git prune -n|--dry-run` and routine `git gc` when effective config does not make expiry immediate. **Specific pre-execution state/probe:** command tokens and effective `gc.pruneExpire` are the cheap gate; only after a match may `git prune -n --expire=now` estimate deletions. A broader read-only `git fsck --unreachable --no-reflogs` found 87,410 unreachable objects (including 1,627 commits), but that is an exposure indicator—not the exact set either command will delete. No local `gc.pruneExpire` override is configured. **Genuinely available:** current object/config state is available, but no preflight can prove another Git process will not create an object after the check. **Noise:** invocation frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** new, justified by collapsing the recovery window after rewrites, stash loss, or ref deletion. **Confidence/decision:** confirmed for installed immediate-prune options, config absence, and measured exposure; inferred for concurrency consequences and actual deletion set. Validate explicit/effective immediate pruning; eliminate ordinary GC/prune and all dry runs. Coalesce a chained immediate reflog-expire plus prune into one advisory. **Race window:** unavoidable object-writer TOCTOU makes “safe now” an invalid guarantee. [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git gc -h`; `git prune -h`; `git config --show-origin --get-all gc.pruneExpire`; `git fsck --unreachable --no-reflogs`]\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-001.md:\n  Line 10: 1. **Direct branch creation — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch <new> [<start>]`, `git checkout -b|-B <new> [<start>]`, or `git switch -c|-C <new> [<start>]` (including direct `git -C <repo> ...` forms). **Pre-execution state:** the command shape is sufficient for the named prohibition; branch existence and registered worktrees are optional refinements obtainable with `git show-ref --verify` and `git worktree list --porcelain`. **Genuinely available:** yes for a direct invocation; no for a Git command hidden in an opaque script or alias expansion. **Noise:** not measured—Git reflogs do not preserve these argv forms, and only 7/1,482 HEAD-reflog entries were checkouts, which is not a branch-creation count. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #2; it requires branch creation through the allocator-backed `git worktree add -b` flow. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule and command classifier; inferred for operational fire rate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:210-221] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-486] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n  Line 12: 2. **Blanket hook bypass — command-only advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git commit ... --no-verify` or `git push ... --no-verify`, accepting option placement before or after the subcommand's ordinary arguments. **Pre-execution state:** only the parsed presence of `--no-verify`; repository state is not needed. **Genuinely available:** yes for direct commands. **Noise:** not measured because reflogs and commit history omit invocation options. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #9; this is especially advisory-worthy because the operation disables the enforcement layer that would otherwise own the check. **Confidence:** confirmed. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-493] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:46-55]\n\n  Line 14: 3. **Amending published or integrated HEAD — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit --amend ...` (including `--amend --no-edit`). **Pre-execution state:** resolve `HEAD`; test whether a local remote-tracking ref or protected/integration branch contains that commit (`git for-each-ref --contains HEAD refs/remotes refs/heads`), and identify the current branch/worktree. **Genuinely available:** locally yes, but only relative to possibly stale remote-tracking refs; whether another clone has consumed the commit is not knowable pre-execution. **Noise:** 7 amend events among 1,482 current-worktree HEAD-reflog entries (0.47%); the hazardous published subset was not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule, NEVER #10. **Confidence:** confirmed for the rule, available local state, and repository count; inferred for publication completeness. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:492-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n  Line 16: 4. **Rebasing published/shared history — stateful advisory, not a generic rebase warning.** **Operation:** `git rebase ...`, `git pull --rebase ...`, or `git pull ...` when `pull.rebase=true`; include an autostash dimension when `--autostash`, `rebase.autoStash=true`, or `merge.autoStash=true` applies. **Pre-execution state:** current branch/detachment, remote-tracking refs containing the commits to rewrite, ahead/behind counts, dirty-path count, unmerged entries, and relevant Git config. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; remote freshness and concurrent consumers remain unknown. **Noise:** 44 rebase events among 1,482 HEAD-reflog entries (2.97%); an unconditional rebase warning would therefore be materially noisier than a published-history-only predicate. The exact autostash subset was not measured because reflogs omit flags/config. **Source classification:** named sk-git rules NEVER #8 and ALWAYS #14; it extends, rather than restates, the briefing incident by gating on publication state as well as dirty-tree/autostash risk. **Confidence:** confirmed for local state and measured broad rate; inferred for the final combined predicate. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:491-493] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n  Line 18: 5. **Discarding worktree/index content — stateful advisory across four exact shapes.** **Operation:** `git reset --hard [<tree-ish>]`, `git clean -f|-fd|-fdx [-- <pathspec>...]`, `git checkout -- <pathspec>...`, or `git restore --worktree [--source=<tree-ish>] [-- <pathspec>...]`. **Pre-execution state:** pathspec-resolved tracked modifications, staged changes that `reset --hard` would replace, matching untracked/ignored paths for `clean`, and unmerged entries. **Genuinely available:** yes via read-only diffs/status plus Git's own pathspec semantics before execution; exact shell-expanded paths must be parsed first. **Noise:** the reflog contains 201 reset events (13.56%), but does not record reset mode, so 13.56% is only a broad upper bound; `clean`, path checkout, and restore frequency were not measured. A warning on every `reset` is ruled out; warn only when the exact command would intersect nonempty at-risk state. **Source classification:** new, justified by immediate loss of uncommitted content and by the high measured noise of the broad family. **Confidence:** confirmed for the broad reset count; inferred for the proposed path-impact predicate. [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`] [INFERENCE: exact at-risk paths can be computed from pre-command index/worktree state, while reset flags are absent from reflog evidence]\n\n  Line 20: 6. **Deleting a local branch with unmerged commits — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git branch -D <branch>...` (not ordinary `-d`, whose merged check is already a Git safety gate). **Pre-execution state:** whether each named branch is checked out in any registered worktree, its configured upstream, and commits unique relative to upstream and the live integration tip. **Genuinely available:** yes locally before execution; absence of a remote branch does not prove no other clone has the commits. **Noise:** not measured because branch deletion argv is not retained in HEAD reflog/history. **Source classification:** new with justification, derived from ALWAYS #17's required worktree-before-branch order and its proof-before-reap model. **Confidence:** inferred; the source confirms the ordering and safety model, not a current advisory. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330] [INFERENCE: `-D` bypasses the merged-branch protection that makes ordinary `-d` lower risk]\n\n  Line 22: 7. **Forced worktree removal — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git worktree remove --force <path>` or `git worktree remove -f <path>`. **Pre-execution state:** target registration, cleanliness from `git -C <path> status --porcelain`, branch merge status against the actual live integration tip, lock state, and the wrapper-session marker/PID when present. **Genuinely available:** yes if the target path and common Git directory remain readable; a missing/unreadable activity marker must remain unknown, not be interpreted as inactive. **Noise:** not measured; Git history does not retain worktree-removal commands. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule ALWAYS #17, which explicitly requires clean, merged, proven-inactive state for automatic removal and otherwise report-only behavior. **Confidence:** confirmed. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:329-330]\n\n  Line 24: 8. **Force-updating or deleting remote branches — command plus local/remote state, with enforcement de-duplication.** **Operation:** `git push [<remote>] --force|--force-with-lease [<refspec>...]`, leading-`+` refspecs, `git push <remote> --delete <branch>...`, or deletion refspecs such as `:<dst>`. **Pre-execution state:** parsed destination refs, resolved remote URL, local expected old SHA/lease, local remote-tracking SHA, allowlist, and whether the destination is `main`/`master`/a protected integration branch. **Genuinely available:** local state yes; authoritative remote tip and server protection require a network read and can race after the check, so they are only partially available. **Noise:** not measured because push argv and rejected pushes do not enter reflogs. **Source classification:** named sk-git rules NEVER #1 and ESCALATE #4 for protected force-push; remote deletion is new with justification. The existing pre-push hook already enforces naming and permission for branch refs, so a new advisory should not duplicate those messages; it should target force/delete semantics the hook does not inspect. **Confidence:** confirmed for current hook coverage and gap; inferred for remote race handling. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-500] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-159]\n\n  Line 26: 9. **Rewriting or deleting release tags — command plus partially remote state.** **Operation:** `git tag -f <tag> [<object>]`, `git tag -d <tag>...`, `git push <remote> --force refs/tags/<tag>`, or `git push <remote> :refs/tags/<tag>`. **Pre-execution state:** whether the local tag exists, annotated/lightweight type, target object, version-like naming, and any locally known remote tag; authoritative remote existence requires `git ls-remote --tags` and can race. **Genuinely available:** local state yes, remote state only partially. **Noise:** not measured. **Source classification:** new with justification: rewriting a release identity is high cost, and current pre-push explicitly skips tags and other non-branch refs. **Confidence:** confirmed for the enforcement gap; inferred for a version-tag-only advisory threshold. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [INFERENCE: limiting the predicate to an existing version-like tag avoids warning on normal first-time tag creation]\n\n  Line 28: 10. **Destroying stash/recovery pointers — stateful advisory only when recovery state is implicated.** **Operation:** `git stash clear`, `git stash drop [<stash>]`, `git reflog expire ... refs/stash`, or an immediate-prune operation while stash/autostash recovery pointers exist. **Pre-execution state:** stash entries and messages, `MERGE_AUTOSTASH`/autostash refs, `refs/autostash-rescue/*`, unmerged index state, and whether the selected commit is reachable from another durable ref. **Genuinely available:** yes before deletion/pruning while the refs still exist. **Noise:** fire frequency not measured; this checkout currently has 20 stash entries, which demonstrates nonempty state but is not an invocation-rate denominator. **Source classification:** named sk-git rule ALWAYS #14 for autostash orphan risk, extended to the exact destructive follow-on commands. **Confidence:** confirmed for named risk and current stash count; inferred for reachability-based suppression. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git stash list`]\n\n  Line 30: 11. **Implicit broad staging by commit — stateful advisory.** **Operation:** `git commit -a|--all ...`. **Pre-execution state:** already-staged paths, modified/deleted tracked paths that `-a` would add, total path count, and top-level ownership spread. **Genuinely available:** yes for the resulting pre-hook index candidate, but whether a path belongs to another session is not encoded in Git and therefore cannot be confirmed. **Noise:** exact `-a` use was not measured; 1,090/1,482 reflog entries (73.55%) are commits, so generic commit warnings are categorically too noisy. **Source classification:** new extension of named scoped-staging discipline and the broad-add incident; it goes beyond `git add <dir>` because `commit -a` performs its own broad tracked-file staging. **Confidence:** confirmed for the available Git sets and broad commit rate; inferred for any ownership/top-level heuristic. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:215-248] [SOURCE: bounded command evidence: `git reflog --format=%gs HEAD`]\n\n  Line 32: 12. **Repository-wide history rewrite or immediate object pruning — command plus recovery-state advisory.** **Operation:** `git filter-branch ...`, `git filter-repo ...`, `git reflog expire --expire=now --all`, `git gc --prune=now`, or `git prune --expire=now`. **Pre-execution state:** clean/dirty status, current refs and worktrees, stash/recovery refs, remote-tracking refs, and objects reachable only through reflogs or rescue refs. **Genuinely available:** most local state is available before execution, although exhaustively proving external consumers or remote recoverability is impossible. **Noise:** not measured; these argv are not retained in normal Git history. **Source classification:** new with justification: these are explicit, rare operations capable of removing the recovery path for rewritten or orphaned commits, including the autostash-loss scenario named by ALWAYS #14. **Confidence:** inferred for advisory design; confirmed only that the named source treats stash drop/clear/GC as the permanent-loss boundary. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:325-327] [INFERENCE: explicit immediate-expiry/prune tokens are quieter classifiers than warning on routine `git gc`]\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-004.md:\n  Line 13: 1. **Detached/linked-worktree reconciliation is predictable as a local-ref consequence, not as push success.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> HEAD:<branch>` (or another source ref targeting `<branch>`) while the current Git directory is detached or belongs to a linked worktree and another registered worktree has local `<branch>` checked out. **Specific pre-execution state:** parse the effective destination; compare absolute `--git-dir` with `--git-common-dir`; read `git worktree list --porcelain`; resolve the source SHA, the other worktree's HEAD, cleanliness, and ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for the local snapshot. A successful remote push will not itself move the other local branch/worktree, but push success and whether a follower advances that tree concurrently remain unknown. **Noise:** exact invocation frequency is unmeasured; current-state exposure is measured at 35 worktrees, 1 detached, and 6 worktree tips not contained in the primary checkout's current HEAD. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #15 plus the observed detached-push incident. **Confidence:** confirmed for Git-directory/worktree state, current counts, and local-ref non-movement; inferred for whether the operator would otherwise miss the work. Retain one advisory only when the exact destination is checked out elsewhere and the source is detached/linked; do not warn on every push. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-330] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git worktree list --porcelain`; per-tip `merge-base --is-ancestor`]\n\n  Line 15: 2. **Push rules must resolve the effective destination before classifying coordination risk.** **Operation:** argument-less `git push`, `git push <remote>` without refspecs, explicit `<src>:<dst>`, and `git -C <repo> push ...`; include `branch.<name>.pushRemote`, upstream, `remote.<name>.push`, `push.default`, push URLs, and URL rewrites. **Specific pre-execution state:** actual cwd/repository, current branch or detachment, branch upstream, all push-specific config, remote/push URL, and parsed refspecs. The current checkout is on `skilled/v4.0.0.0` with matching `origin` upstream and `push.autoSetupRemote=true`, but several other local branches are configured to merge differently named or shared release refs, proving that local branch text is not a universal destination proxy. **Genuinely available before execution:** local configuration and explicit mappings are available; ambiguous Git expansion can be reproduced only by faithfully implementing Git's precedence or using a dry run that may contact the remote and invoke hooks. **Noise:** exact push-shape frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new prerequisite justified by destination-sensitive advisories. **Confidence:** confirmed for documented precedence and current config; inferred for the cheapest complete resolver. Remain silent rather than guessing when destination expansion is ambiguous. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `git config --get-regexp` for remote, branch, and push keys]\n\n  Line 17: 3. **A manual live-branch push from a wrapper session bypasses the safer autosync primitive and merits a narrow advisory.** **Operation:** direct `git push origin HEAD:<live>` from a linked `work/<runtime>/<slug>` session when `SPECKIT_AUTOSYNC=1` and `<live> == $SPECKIT_LIVE_BRANCH`; exclude `bash .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh`, whose nested push is not the direct PreToolUse command. **Specific pre-execution state:** linked-worktree identity, current branch, wrapper namespace, the two environment variables, destination branch, tracked cleanliness, and source/live ancestry. **Genuinely available before execution:** yes for a direct command and environment snapshot. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured; current session is `AI_SESSION_CHILD=1` in the primary checkout with both autosync variables unset, so this predicate would not fire now. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #16, extended to command classification. **Confidence:** confirmed. `git-sync.sh` fetches, retries push races, rebases only a clean tracked tree, and aborts conflicts; the hand-rolled push skips that coordination. The pre-push permission exemption is not evidence that manual publication is safe—it exists so the scripted primitive can publish without blocking. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:327-329] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/continuous-integration.md:78-103] [SOURCE: bounded environment evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n  Line 19: 4. **Active `gh` account versus remote owner is positive identity evidence, not a Git-push authorization oracle.** **Operation:** `gh pr create`, `gh release create`, and—with lower confidence—`git push` to a GitHub HTTPS remote. **Specific pre-execution state:** resolve the effective push URL and owner; query the active host account (`gh api user`) and repository permission snapshot; separately inspect which credential helper/transport Git will use. Current evidence is positive: remote owner `MichelKerkmeester`, active account `MichelKerkmeester`, and GitHub reports `push=true`/`admin=true` for that authenticated API viewer. **Genuinely available before execution:** identity and API permission are network snapshots. They are directly relevant to `gh` commands; they do not prove which credential Git's HTTPS/SSH transport will present, branch-protection bypass, server-hook acceptance, or future validity. **Noise:** mismatch frequency is unmeasured; the predicate is false in the current checkout. **Source classification:** observed 403 incident, refined beyond a string mismatch. **Confidence:** confirmed for current identity/permission and the limitation; inferred for a generic Git credential relationship. Advise on a positive owner/account mismatch for `gh`; for `git push`, require transport/credential evidence or phrase it only as “account differs,” never “push will 403.” [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:21-32] [SOURCE: bounded network evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z: `gh api user`; `gh api repos/MichelKerkmeester/opencode--skilled-agent-loops-with-spec-kit-memory`]\n\n  Line 21: 5. **Force-with-lease forms have materially different preflight strength.** **Operation:** `git push ... --force`, `-f`, leading-`+` refspecs, `--force-with-lease`, `--force-with-lease=<ref>`, and `--force-with-lease=<ref>:<expect>`; include `--force-if-includes`. **Specific pre-execution state:** exact destinations, local source SHA, explicit expected SHA when present, local remote-tracking tips, background-fetch risk, and whether multiple refs are selected. **Genuinely available before execution:** command tokens and local refs are available. Only the explicit `<ref>:<expect>` form fixes the intended expected value in the command; acceptance is still an execution-time server comparison. Bare lease forms use remote-tracking refs and can be weakened by background fetch, while `--force` applies to every selected ref. **Noise:** option-specific use is unmeasured; ordinary history cannot recover push argv or rejected pushes. **Source classification:** named NEVER #1 / ESCALATE #4, refined with official lease semantics. **Confidence:** confirmed. Recommend an advisory for unconditional force, leading `+`, or a broad lease without explicit expectation when the destination would retreat/rewrite known history; lower or suppress it for explicit leases with no positive rewrite evidence. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:482-500] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`]\n\n  Line 23: 6. **Remote deletion has quiet exact shapes and broad high-cost variants.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> --delete <ref>...`, empty-source refspecs `:<dst>`, `--prune` with a mapping, and `--mirror` (which also force-updates and deletes refs). **Specific pre-execution state:** resolved destination namespaces; positive local remote-tracking/tag snapshots; destination checked-out/integration status; ref count selected by prune/mirror; and allowlist/hook coverage. **Genuinely available before execution:** local mappings and known refs are available, but the authoritative remote set is a raceable network snapshot and server deletion policy is unknown. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new beyond the briefing, justified by irreversible shared-ref removal and the batch reach of prune/mirror. **Confidence:** confirmed for command semantics; inferred for remote impact from stale local refs. Retain exact delete/mirror/prune advisories only when at least one known destination exists; never claim the deletion will be accepted. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [SOURCE: installed Git 2.50.1 `git push -h`] [INFERENCE: positive local destination evidence is sufficient to describe intent but not authoritative remote outcome]\n\n  Line 25: 7. **Tag force/delete operations are outside both current pre-push gates.** **Operation:** `git push <remote> --force refs/tags/<tag>`, `+refs/tags/<tag>:refs/tags/<tag>`, `git push <remote> :refs/tags/<tag>`, `--delete <tag>` when it resolves to a tag, and force combined with `--tags`. **Specific pre-execution state:** destination namespace, local tag existence/type/target, version-like naming, known remote-tracking/tag snapshot, and whether the source changes an existing target. The checkout currently has 143 local tags. **Genuinely available before execution:** local tag facts are available; authoritative remote target and release consumers are not. **Noise:** invocation frequency is unmeasured; 143 tags measure exposure, not fire rate. **Source classification:** new enforcement-gap refinement. **Confidence:** confirmed. The hook's `refs/heads/*` case skips every tag; a fabricated tag-deletion input returned 0. Advise only on force/delete/mirror semantics with positive known tag evidence, not ordinary first-time tag publication. [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-88] [SOURCE: bounded hook matrix and tag inventory captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n  Line 27: 8. **Multi-ref and multi-remote destructive pushes can partially land unless atomicity is explicitly and successfully negotiated.** **Operation:** one push selecting multiple refs through multiple refspecs, `--all`, `--tags`, `--mirror`, or `--prune`; and a configured remote group that expands into sequential independent pushes. **Specific pre-execution state:** expanded destination count, destructive/force members, presence of `--atomic`, remote-group membership, and local remote capability knowledge. **Genuinely available before execution:** command/config expansion is locally available; server support for atomic push and per-remote outcomes are execution-time facts. Official Git behavior makes remote-group members independent and rejects `--atomic` for a group. **Noise:** exact multi-ref/group invocation frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** new high-cost coordination rule beyond the five incidents. **Confidence:** confirmed for semantics; inferred for expected prevalence. Advise only when multiple selected destinations include force/delete/mirror/prune or when a remote group makes partial cross-remote publication possible; generic multi-ref publication would be noisy. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [INFERENCE: destructive multi-destination selection creates materially higher partial-outcome cost than an ordinary single-ref push]\n\n  Line 29: 9. **Permission/naming advisories duplicate execution-time blocking logic and should be silent—but current installation is contradictory evidence, not permission to duplicate.** **Operation:** creating/updating a non-allowlisted `refs/heads/*` on `origin`, or creating a malformed/wrapper branch. **Specific pre-execution state:** destination branch, new/update status, allowlist, bypass variables, autosync exact-live exception, and installed-hook presence. The versioned hook blocks a fabricated conformant nonallowlisted branch, allows `skilled/v*`, allows only the exact autosync live branch, and blocks a different branch. However, `core.hooksPath` currently resolves to `.git/hooks` and `.git/hooks/pre-push` is absent. **Genuinely available before execution:** all local inputs and hook installation are available; the real pre-push hook's remote SHA arrives later at push execution. **Noise:** exact blocked-push frequency is unmeasured. **Source classification:** named remote-push policy and current-state contradiction. **Confidence:** confirmed. The advisory layer should stay silent for the same naming/permission messages because the blocking hook owns them and prints the retry; `--no-verify` remains separately advisory-worthy because it deliberately bypasses that owner. A missing installation belongs to the existing SessionStart hook-health warning, not a repeated per-push imitation. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/remote-branch-policy.md:21-96] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-push:77-163] [SOURCE: bounded installed-hook and fabricated-stdin evidence captured 2026-07-27T19:32:26Z]\n\n  Line 31: 10. **The exact preflight boundary is local positive evidence; authorization and final remote outcomes remain raceable.** **Operation:** every retained push advisory above. **Specific pre-execution state:** direct command text, cwd/`-C`, effective local config, current refs, worktree registry, environment, hook installation, and optionally an authenticated remote/API snapshot. **Genuinely available before execution:** local snapshots are genuinely available; shell-generated arguments, concurrent ref/worktree changes, credentials actually selected by transport, server hooks/protection, force-lease comparison, atomic capability, and push result are not final until execution. **Noise:** push argv/failure telemetry is absent, so all option-specific historical fire rates are explicitly unmeasured; measured current facts (35 worktrees, 6 tips outside primary HEAD, 143 tags, matching owner/account, missing installed pre-push) are exposure measurements only. **Source classification:** synthesis of local source, current state, and official remote semantics. **Confidence:** confirmed for the boundary; inferred for eventual attention cost. One advisory should coalesce all positive facts per command, and absent/mismatched snapshots must never be translated into “safe,” “authorized,” “will fail,” or “will succeed.” [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/finish-workflows.md:329-372] [SOURCE: .opencode/bin/git-sync.sh:90-155] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-push] [INFERENCE: a pre-execution snapshot cannot bind concurrent local writers or the remote receive transaction]\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-005.md:\n  Line 23: | Rank | Candidate and exact operation | Pre-state and genuine availability | Noise / source / confidence | Cost, probe, overlap |\n\n  Line 51: | Rejected candidate / exact operation | Pre-state and availability | Noise / source / confidence | Rejection |\n\n\n/Users/michelkerkmeester/MEGA/Development/Code_Environment/Public/.opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/lineages/sol/iterations/iteration-003.md:\n  Line 13: 1. **Broad add needs an objective delta, not an ownership accusation.** **Operation:** `git add -A|--all [--] [<pathspec>...]`, `git add .`, or `git add <directory>`; resolve global `-C <repo>` and the command cwd before interpreting `.`. **Specific pre-execution state:** snapshot `S0 = git diff --cached --name-only -z`, then run the same selection as `git add -n`/`--dry-run` to obtain candidate updates `A`; report only facts such as “index already has |S0| paths; broad add would update |A| more.” In the present primary checkout, `S0=0`, `git add -n -A -- .` reported 151 candidate entries, and `git add -n -u -- .` reported 47. **Genuine pre-run availability:** the sets are available to a stateful PreToolUse process with the actual cwd; the current evaluator receives only command text, and Git does not encode session ownership. **Noise:** exact broad-add invocation frequency is not measured; current exposure is measured. A rule requiring a nonempty pre-existing index would stay silent now despite 149 dirty paths, so it is precise but incomplete. **Source classification:** named sk-git scoped-staging rule (commit workflow Step 7 / ALWAYS #13) plus the observed add-directory incident. **Confidence:** confirmed for command semantics, current counts, and lack of ownership state; inferred for the best threshold. Recommend an advisory only for exact broad shapes with a material objective delta, phrased neutrally; silence when the only claim would be cross-session ownership. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:215-248] [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:21-32] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git add -h`; `git add -n -A -- .`; `git add -n -u -- .`; `git diff --cached --name-only -z`]\n\n  Line 15: 2. **`commit -a` is a separate broad-staging operation, but path count alone is a noisy false friend.** **Operation:** `git commit -a|--all ...` (including `--amend -a`), excluding `--dry-run`. **Specific pre-execution state:** current staged set, all modified/deleted tracked paths that `-a` would add, unmerged entries, worktree identity, and native `git commit --dry-run --porcelain` with the same selection options. The current checkout has 0 staged, 46 unstaged tracked, and 103 untracked paths; `-a` would consider the tracked modifications/deletions but not the untracked paths. **Genuine pre-run availability:** those sets and whether this is the primary or a linked worktree are locally available; which tracked edits belong to another session is not. The current pure evaluator cannot obtain them without a stateful probe layer. **Noise:** option-specific use is not measured. In the latest 500 commits, 453 (90.6%) touched at least 2 paths, 389 (77.8%) at least 4, and 287 (57.4%) at least 10, so “many paths” is demonstrably normal commit history and cannot by itself justify a warning. **Source classification:** new extension of the named scoped-staging rule, justified because Git performs implicit tracked-file staging at commit time. **Confidence:** confirmed for Git semantics, current state, and history rates; inferred for contamination risk. Retain only an exact `-a` advisory with objective dirty-set context in a shared/primary tree; do not warn merely because the candidate contains many paths. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit/2.50.0] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:215-248] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: 500-commit `git log --name-status --find-renames`; current `git diff`/`git ls-files` counts]\n\n  Line 17: 3. **Path arguments silently select `--only` semantics even when `--only` is absent.** **Operation:** `git commit [--only|-o] [options] -- <pathspec>...` and ordinary `git commit [options] <pathspec>...`; special no-path forms `git commit --only --amend` and `git commit --only --allow-empty` are distinct. **Specific pre-execution state:** resolve the pathspec against HEAD/index/worktree, snapshot staged paths outside the selected set, and use `git commit --dry-run --porcelain` with the same options to display selected versus held-back paths. Git documents that named paths use working-tree contents, disregard staged contents for other paths, and leave those held-back paths staged. **Genuine pre-run availability:** the probable pre-hook selection and holdback set are available before execution; whether holdback is intentional is not. **Noise:** exact path-commit frequency and omitted-request rate are not measured; the observed dropped-named-path incident proves cost but not prevalence. **Source classification:** observed `commit --only` incident, extended by official Git semantics showing that explicit path arguments imply the same behavior without spelling `--only`. **Confidence:** confirmed for operation semantics and probe availability; inferred for operator intent. Warn only when the native dry-run reveals a surprising condition—requested changed paths absent or a nonempty staged set held back—and state the sets without saying the omission is accidental. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit/2.50.0] [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:21-32] [INFERENCE: comparing the parsed requested pathspec, HEAD-relative changes, and Git's own dry-run is the narrowest preflight test for a named path that would not enter the candidate commit]\n\n  Line 19: 4. **`--include` is the opposite path-commit mode and usually should remain silent.** **Operation:** `git commit -i|--include <pathspec>...`; do not confuse `-i` here with `git add -i`. **Specific pre-execution state:** existing staged paths, selected tracked worktree paths, unmerged entries, and merge/cherry-pick state. Git says this stages the named paths *in addition to* staged contents and is usually used to conclude a conflicted merge. **Genuine pre-run availability:** all local sets and operation markers are available, but ownership and the final conflict-resolution intent are not. **Noise:** invocation frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** new missed case with justification: it can combine more than the explicit path list, but the behavior is explicit and designed for conflict completion. **Confidence:** confirmed for Git semantics; inferred for attention cost. Recommend silence by default; at most reuse the neutral “pre-existing index plus selected paths” summary when a separate scoped-staging rule already has positive evidence. [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit/2.50.0] [INFERENCE: an unconditional advisory would restate an explicit mode and distract during conflict recovery]\n\n  Line 21: 5. **Pathspec cardinality is probeable only after respecting the shell/Git boundary.** **Operation:** quoted Git patterns such as `git add -- '*.md'`, pathspec magic such as `git add -- ':(glob)**/*.md'`, directory pathspecs, unquoted shell globs such as `git add *.md`, and `git add|commit --pathspec-from-file=<file> [--pathspec-file-nul]`; stdin form `--pathspec-from-file=-` is separate. **Specific pre-execution state:** actual cwd, quoting/token boundaries, preexisting readable pathspec-file bytes, Git pathspec expansion, candidate set, and top-level spread. **Genuine pre-run availability:** quoted Git pathspecs and a stable regular file are reproducible with a dry-run; unquoted shell expansion has not happened when the Bash PreToolUse hook sees the command string, and stdin/pipeline-generated pathspec content may not yet exist. Emulating arbitrary shell expansion or consuming stdin is not a cheap safe probe. **Noise:** exact invocation rates are not measured; 46/500 commits (9.2%) legitimately touched at least two top-level roots, so cross-root expansion is evidence for review, not contamination. **Source classification:** new with justification, because broadness can be hidden behind patterns or a pathspec file rather than `.`/a directory token. **Confidence:** confirmed for Git's pathspec-file options and the measured history proxy; inferred for shell-emulation limits. Probe only reproducible direct forms; remain silent on ownership and on non-reproducible stdin/interactive selections. [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git add -h`; installed Git 2.50.1] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit/2.50.0] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: 500-commit top-level spread]\n\n  Line 23: 6. **Partial rename staging is detectable as a heuristic pair, not as index ownership.** **Operation:** `git add [--] <old-path>` or `<new-path>`, a directory/pathspec selecting only one side of a worktree rename, and broad add shapes whose candidate set intersects only one side after exclusions. **Specific pre-execution state:** `git diff --name-status -M` rename/copy pairs, current `git diff --cached --name-status -M`, and the dry-run-selected paths; flag only a pair where exactly one side would change index state. The current checkout has no inferred worktree or staged rename entry. **Genuine pre-run availability:** rename similarity and selected paths are available, but Git records snapshots and infers renames during diff; a preflight cannot prove semantic identity or another session's ownership. Exactly simulating the post-add index would require a temporary alternate index, which is heavier than the cheap read-only probes. **Noise:** 36/500 commits (7.2%) contained an inferred rename/copy, while one-sided staging frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** named sk-git ALWAYS #10 (“confirm R-status”), extended from the observed half-rename incident. **Confidence:** confirmed for current absence and history rate; inferred for pair-level prediction. Emit only a neutral one-line “selected one side of inferred rename old → new” advisory; do not warn on every rename, and do not treat delete+add as proof of an error. [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/SKILL.md:321-323] [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:21-32] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: current and 500-commit `git diff|log --name-status --find-renames`]\n\n  Line 25: 7. **Forced ignored-file staging is a quieter missed case; interactive and intent-only staging are false friends.** **Operation:** retain `git add -f|--force <pathspec>` when the candidate includes ignored files; exclude `git add -n|--dry-run`, `git add -N|--intent-to-add`, and `git add -p|--patch|-i|--interactive|-e|--edit` from content-sweep advisories. **Specific pre-execution state:** exact dry-run candidate set plus `git check-ignore -v --no-index` for ignored candidates; for interactive/edit modes, only the offered set exists before the operator chooses hunks. **Genuine pre-run availability:** ignored status and direct path candidates are available; the final interactive/edit selection is not. **Noise:** invocation frequency is not measured. **Source classification:** new with justification: `-f` deliberately crosses ignore protection and can expose generated artifacts or secrets, while the excluded modes are dry-run, metadata-only, or operator-selection flows. **Confidence:** confirmed for installed option semantics; inferred for risk classification. Advise only when `-f` has a nonempty ignored candidate set, naming count/path class without exposing content; remain silent for the excluded modes. [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: `git add -h`] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/sk-git/references/commit-workflows.md:288-305] [INFERENCE: a positive ignored-file match is materially narrower than warning on every add command]\n\n  Line 27: 8. **PreToolUse can describe only the pre-hook candidate; hooks own a later mutable boundary.** **Operation:** any `git commit` selection mode, especially `git commit --only <paths>`, `git commit -a`, and plain `git commit`; `--no-verify` separately bypasses the current `pre-commit` and `commit-msg` gates. **Specific pre-execution state:** command text, cwd, HEAD, worktree/index snapshots, installed hook files, and Git's dry-run candidate. The current outer pre-commit and nested comment-hygiene hook inspect `git diff --cached`; the commit-msg hook reads staged-path count and blocks a bodyless message at four or more paths. Their inspected bodies contain no direct `git add` or `git update-index`, but they invoke validators, so only the inspected direct layer is confirmed read-only. **Genuine pre-run availability:** all of that is inspectable before `git commit`; changes made after command start—by hook code, concurrent processes, or interactive message/patch flows—are not a guaranteed PreToolUse fact. Git runs commit hooks after entry to `git commit`, and hook implementations are allowed to alter repository state unless project policy forbids it. **Noise:** hook-induced index mutation was not observed or measured in repository history; ordinary history cannot record it. **Source classification:** new boundary finding required by evaluator/hook architecture, with current-hook evidence. **Confidence:** confirmed for current direct hook reads, hook ordering surface, and the command-only evaluator; inferred for possible child-hook mutation. Advisory wording must say “would be selected before hooks,” never “the commit will contain exactly.” [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit:17-118] [SOURCE: .opencode/hooks/pre-commit:7-58] [SOURCE: .opencode/scripts/git-hooks/commit-msg:145-175] [SOURCE: .opencode/skills/cli-external-orchestration/cli-opencode/scripts/lib/dispatch-rule-checks.mjs:68-125] [SOURCE: https://git-scm.com/docs/git-commit/2.50.0]\n\n  Line 29: 9. **The cheap discriminator is set comparison plus isolation evidence; there is no reliable ownership probe.** **Operation:** run only after matching an exact broad/selective shape: broad `git add`, `git commit -a`, or path-limited `git commit [--only] <pathspec>`. **Specific pre-execution state:** (1) `git status --porcelain=v2 -z`, (2) staged names, (3) unstaged tracked names, (4) untracked names only when the operation can include them, (5) native add/commit dry-run with identical pathspecs, and (6) registered worktree/current-worktree identity. Compare baseline index to candidate, coalesce to one line, and cap path samples. **Genuine pre-run availability:** objective sets, counts, rename heuristics, and worktree isolation are available if cwd is trustworthy; session ownership, author intent, and later hook mutation are unavailable. **Noise:** real-history proxies show 90.6% of 500 commits were multi-path, 77.8% had at least four paths, 57.4% had at least ten, and 9.2% crossed top-level roots. Therefore thresholds based only on 2/4/10 paths or multiple roots would fire on normal intentional work. Exact advisory fire rate is not measured because argv telemetry does not exist. **Source classification:** new synthesis justified by the briefing's noise requirement and measured repository history. **Confidence:** confirmed for probe inputs and history rates; inferred for the recommended gate. Recommend silence unless an exact broad/selective command combines with a concrete mismatch (held-back staged paths, one-sided inferred rename, ignored file forced in, or reproducible expansion materially beyond explicit paths). [SOURCE: .opencode/specs/sk-git/016-git-action-advisory-hook/001-advisory-research/research/BRIEFING.md:38-59] [SOURCE: bounded Git evidence: current status/diff/dry-run probes and 500-commit history sample] [INFERENCE: positive mismatches are less noisy than cardinality or directory breadth alone]\n","metadata":{"matches":44,"truncated":false},"title":"\\*\\*Operation:\\*\\*|\\| Operation \\| Pre-state|Pre-state and genuine availability|Noise / source / confidence","time":{"start":1785181839261,"end":1785181839290}},"metadata":{"openai":{"itemId":"fc_02fb8a138c9380a4016a67b68eed4481919894f20ad3550029"}},"id":"prt_fa5211f6c001ctgSOQmXQwrVK2","sessionID":"ses_05b144ddbffeTP8bqXDQv88HuN","messageID":"msg_fa520dc52001cIctQuYqyqgDE7"}}
